window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol4/01_boundary"] = {"key": "vol4/01_boundary", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "Artificial Intelligence Beyond Glass"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "From Prediction to Action"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Delay consumes stopping clearance"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 3, "title": "Irreversibility and recovery"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 2, "title": "Three Machine Classes"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 2, "title": "The Physical AI Scope Test"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 2, "title": "The Machine in Five Levels"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 3, "title": "Tracking history loss under classification churn"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 3, "title": "Action suppression and lost stopping distance"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 2, "title": "Where Safety, Security, and Accountability Meet"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 2, "title": "Summary"}, {"id": "sec-13", "level": 2, "title": "Historical Perspective and Further Reading"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">The Causal Boundary</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why can a better prediction still produce a worse physical action?</em></p>\n<p>A learned model can recognize a task correctly and still act too late to stop a moving machine or prevent excessive contact force. Once a command produces motion or heat, replacing the prediction cannot undo the work already delivered. The engineer must therefore connect model quality to the body's limits, the time available to respond, and the evidence supporting the next action. An independent permission path must be able to refuse a proposal and carry out a feasible fallback even when the model stalls. At the causal boundary, where commands become physical work, irreversibility makes these responsibilities part of the system's architecture.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; pagebreak &gt;}}</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Trace how machine learning systems evolved from disembodied prediction through digital agency to physical embodiment, and explain why that progression gives data, compute, evaluation, and deployment physical stakes</li>\n<li>Explain why an action that crosses the causal boundary requires reasoning about consequences that software rollback cannot undo</li>\n<li>Calculate how response delay consumes a moving machine's stopping clearance</li>\n<li>Apply the scope test to distinguish physical AI from advisory learning and classical control</li>\n<li>Distinguish learned proposals from permission to execute them in the five-level machine</li>\n<li>Diagnose how perception, response timing, and missing redundancy interacted in the Tempe collision</li>\n<li>Relate the four laws to their established names in the field and to the book's four parts</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">Artificial Intelligence Beyond Glass</h2>\n<p>When an image classifier mislabels a warehouse photograph on a server, a downstream filter can discard the label before any machine moves; when a mobile robot's policy misjudges aisle clearance and drives its wheel motors, the chassis is already accelerating toward the rack. Across offline advisory prediction, digital agency, and physical embodiment, computation acquires different consequences as its outputs enter an action loop, and physical AI diverges from the other two in how agency, time, and recovery behave (@tbl-01-paradigms).</p>\n<p>In the offline or advisory case, a model delivers predictions against a static dataset before any downstream action closes a feedback loop. Whether classifying an image, predicting customer churn, estimating a bounding box, or ranking search results, the model operates in open loop. Its input distribution is exogenous, because the predictions it emits do not alter the process that generates later test examples. Because those requests arrive independently, throughput-oriented serving can batch many inputs together ($\\BatchSize \\gg 1$) across pipeline stages, performing many arithmetic operations for every byte of weights fetched from memory (its arithmetic intensity). The advisory model exercises no agency of its own. Before a person or downstream service acts on the output, an incorrect label or irrelevant search result can be ignored, filtered, or recalculated without having done physical work.</p>\n<p>The emergence of agentic machine learning marked an architectural transition from passive, single-shot prediction to active, sequential decision-making. Endowed with reasoning loops and test-time deliberation, an agentic model formulates multi-step plans, maintains internal state across execution traces, and autonomously invokes external tools. These tools execute database queries, call web APIs, run generated code in execution environments, and coordinate software subagents. In this regime, the execution loop is closed. An action taken at step $\\TurnIndex$ alters the state of the software environment, so subsequent observations $\\TrajectoryObservation_{t+1}$ depend directly on prior decisions $\\TrajectoryAction_t$. The system possesses genuine agency.</p>\n<p>Within a controlled software environment, this agency can operate inside a protective envelope, \"behind the glass.\" Such environments can grant agentic systems three structural privileges, though external services and consequential tool calls may fall outside that envelope:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Elastic virtual time</strong>: A controlled software environment can wait while the model computes. Whether an agent requires $200\\text{ ms}$ or $30\\text{ s}$ of test-time compute to plan its next tool call, the environment can hold its simulated state until the action is emitted. External services continue evolving on their own clocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Transactional rollback and sandboxing</strong>: Tool execution can occur within isolated containers, virtual machines, or transactional databases. When an action produces an error inside this envelope, software exceptions catch the fault, database transactions roll back, or the container restarts from a clean snapshot. These mechanisms cannot undo consequences already delivered outside it.</li>\n<li><strong>Discrete symbolic interfaces</strong>: Actions are structured strings, JSON schemas, or API payloads whose syntax and validity can be verified by deterministic compilers and linters before execution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Physical artificial intelligence represents the arrival of agency in the physical world. A physical AI system does not invoke a software API inside a virtual sandbox; its outputs cross the <strong>causal boundary</strong> into physical actuators. A learned model's output becomes a setpoint (the target value a low-level controller drives toward), the setpoint becomes electrical current in a motor phase winding (one of the stationary copper coils whose current turns the motor), and the current becomes electromagnetic torque. The torque accelerates physical mass through gearboxes, linkages, and contact surfaces sharing space with people and objects. That physical embodiment strips away every digital privilege:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Continuous time</strong>: Physical time advances under Newtonian mechanics regardless of processor state. The <strong>physical plant</strong>, the apparatus being controlled such as a robot's wheeled base or arm, does not pause while a neural network deliberates. In a moving robot, computational latency is not a software delay. A machine moving at speed $\\BodySpeed$ that takes a time $\\PrebrakeDelay$ to begin braking covers $\\BodySpeed\\,\\PrebrakeDelay$ toward an obstacle under unguided momentum. Delay is physical distance.</li>\n<li><strong>Mechanical and thermodynamic irreversibility</strong>: Physical state transitions admit no software rollback. Once software commands current through an inverter (the power electronics that switch a DC supply into the motor's phase currents), electromagnetic force accelerates mass, dissipating irreversible Joule heat ($\\PhaseJoulePower = \\MotorPhaseRmsCurrent^2 \\PhaseResistance$ in each phase winding) and altering mechanical contact states. Kinetic energy ($\\KineticEnergy = \\frac{1}{2}\\Mass \\BodySpeed^2$) cannot be garbage-collected, and a mechanical impact cannot be undone by restoring a checkpoint. A command that has moved a machine can be answered only by another physical action, which itself consumes time, clearance, and energy.</li>\n<li><strong>Single-batch, hard real-time execution</strong>: On the running machine, each new action proposal depends on observations shaped by prior motion, so the live loop does not accumulate independent observations into a throughput batch ($\\BatchSize=1$). Control loops must also meet hard real-time deadlines across several coupled timescales: motor current loops at tens of kilohertz, loops near a kilohertz that check motion and regulate contact force, learned policies renewing at tens of hertz, and slower models interpreting the scene a few times per second. @Sec-boundary-machine-model gives the running machine's rates. A missed deadline does not degrade user experience; it can destabilize the physical plant.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Losing these privileges forces an architectural response. Deep neural networks are statistical function approximators, vulnerable to distribution shift, sensor noise, and hallucination, so they cannot be granted unmediated authority over physical actuators. Physical AI therefore separates proposal from permission. High-capacity learned models act only as <em>proposers</em> in an unprivileged tier (the Brain). An independent, deterministic <em>permission path</em> (in the Nervous System) checks each proposal against the machine's physical limits and <em>admits</em> (accepts for execution) only the proposals that pass. While keeping an executable fallback ready on isolated real-time hardware, the permission path completes these checks before any action crosses the causal boundary into the plant (the Body).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Deployment Regime</th><th>Execution Substrate</th><th>Primary Output</th><th>Environmental Coupling</th><th>Time Semantics</th><th>Failure &amp; Recovery</th><th>Safety &amp; Authority Model</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Offline/advisory prediction</strong></td><td>Cloud servers / accelerators</td><td>Advisory prediction (label, score, token)</td><td>Open-loop (static dataset, exogenous data)</td><td>Batchable in the selected serving case</td><td>Discard or recompute before downstream action</td><td>Advisory; downstream software or human decides</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Agentic ML</strong></td><td>Digital software environments</td><td>Tool execution (API payload, code snippet)</td><td>Closed-loop in software (actions alter state)</td><td>Elastic virtual time in controlled environments</td><td>Software exception, container restart, DB rollback</td><td>Sandboxed permission; API scopes and virtual gates</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Physical AI</strong></td><td>Embodied physical plant</td><td>Physical force and motion (current, torque)</td><td>Closed-loop in physics (actions alter world)</td><td>Inexorable continuous time; delay is distance</td><td>Irreversible work; energy dissipation; damage</td><td>Proposal vs. permission; deterministic runtime veto</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Three deployment regimes of machine learning systems</strong>: The selected arrangements progress from offline advisory prediction to digital agency to physical embodiment. The unit of engineering shifts from advisory prediction to digital tool execution, and finally to irreversible physical action across the causal boundary. {#tbl-01-paradigms tbl-colwidths=\"[14,14,14,14,14,15,15]\"}</p>\n<p>Physical AI did not arrive solely through the scaling of language models. It represents the convergence of two long-standing engineering traditions: robotics and control, which learned to act reliably on a physical world that pushes back, and machine learning, which learned to extract representations from data at scale. Over eighty years, the learned model has moved step by step from the periphery of the machine toward its actuators, and each step shifted what the surrounding systems architecture had to guarantee. That history explains what physical AI inherits from classical control and digital learning, and why its central systems challenge is new.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">From Prediction to Action</h2>\n<p>The acting line begins with feedback. Wiener's cybernetics described animals and machines alike as systems that measure the result of their own action and use it to correct the next one [@wiener1948cybernetics], and control theory soon learned to estimate a moving system's state from noisy measurements [@kalman1960new]. The first industrial robots needed little of this at the level of the task. In 1961 a Unimate arm began stacking hot die-cast parts at General Motors by replaying step-by-step commands stored on a magnetic drum [@chm1961unimate]. Nothing in that program could notice a person in the arm's path, so protecting people meant keeping them out of its reach. The machine acted, but it did not perceive, and nothing in it was learned. Robotics and control learning to act and machine learning mastering prediction run as the upper and lower lanes of @fig-01-evolution until they merge.</p>\n<p>Shakey, developed at SRI from 1966 through 1972, joined perception to planning [@nilsson1984shakey]. It took camera images and range-finder readings, kept a model of its surroundings, and used a planner called STRIPS to compose sequences of actions that would achieve a stated goal. This organization, later summarized as sense-plan-act (S·P·A), treats acting as the last step of a computation. Shakey moved slowly, and its designers treated its motion as running asynchronously to the programs directing it [@nilsson1984shakey]. The world was assumed to wait while the computer thought. Shakey also carried a detail that anticipates this book's architecture. When one of its catwhiskers touched an obstacle, the robot stopped by a reflex action beneath the planner.</p>\n<p>↪ The sense-plan-act paradigm, and the failure taxonomy it organizes, is developed in @sec-appendix-spa.</p>\n<p>Brooks inverted that organization. His subsumption architecture built control from layers of simple, asynchronous behaviors, each coupling sensing to action. Higher layers could suppress the outputs of lower ones, but the lower layers continued to run as higher ones were added [@brooks1986robust]. A fast layer that keeps working while a slow one is busy or wrong is an ancestor of the permission path this book develops. Neither organization, however, made perception or dexterous motion easy to write by hand, and the planners and behavior layers alike stalled on exactly the skills a toddler has. That difficulty has a name. <strong>Moravec's paradox</strong> observes that sensorimotor skills people perform with little conscious effort can be difficult to reproduce computationally, which is why learning became attractive for physical tasks.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Moravec's Paradox</strong>: @moravec1988mind observed that computers could be made to perform at an adult level on intelligence tests or at checkers far more easily than they could be given a one-year-old's perception and mobility, and he traced the gap to evolutionary age, since sensorimotor skill was refined over a far longer history than abstract reasoning. For a physical machine the hard part is therefore the part that touches the causal boundary, perceiving a changing world and acting on it before a deadline, which is where learning and this book both concentrate.</p>\n<p>Learning had already entered the loop. In 1989 ALVINN, a small neural network, mapped camera and laser range images to the direction a vehicle should steer, and followed real roads under some field conditions [@pomerleau1989alvinn]. Through the 1990s robotics also made uncertainty explicit. Probabilistic robotics represents what a machine believes about itself and its surroundings as probability distributions, updated as actions and measurements arrive [@thrun2005probabilistic]. The DARPA Grand Challenge tested these ideas at scale. In the first race, in 2004, no vehicle covered more than a small fraction of the desert course. In 2005 five vehicles finished, and the winner, Stanford's Stanley, relied predominantly on machine learning and probabilistic reasoning [@thrun2006stanley]. In systems of this kind, learning mostly estimated what the world was like, and hand-written planners and controllers decided what to do. A decade later, the DARPA Robotics Challenge Finals asked robots to work at a simulated disaster site, driving a vehicle, opening a door, turning a valve, and climbing stairs. The organizers deliberately degraded the communications link to the remote human operators, so that each robot had to carry out parts of every task under supervised autonomy rather than direct teleoperation [@krotkov2017darpa]. Even at that stage, a person remained close to the loop.</p>\n<p>↪ ALVINN's missing recovery examples, and the compounding error they expose, are developed in @sec-data-curse-dimensionality-recoverability.</p>\n<p>Deep learning then changed how much a machine could perceive. In 2012 a deep convolutional network trained on GPUs classified ImageNet photographs with error well below the previous state of the art [@krizhevsky2012imagenet], and learned detectors spread into the perception systems of robots and automated vehicles. The architecture around them usually stayed modular, with a learned percept feeding a planner written as rules or as an optimization. The learned outputs were still predictions, yet no person stood between them and the brakes. The Tempe vehicle examined in @sec-boundary-when-fails was a machine of this kind.</p>\n<p>Learning reached the actuator itself in the following years. Levine and colleagues trained convolutional networks that mapped raw camera images directly to the torques at a robot's motors, for tasks such as screwing a cap onto a bottle [@levine2016endtoend]. Grasping systems learned from hundreds of thousands of their own attempts, gathered by groups of robot arms working in parallel [@levine2018learning; @kalashnikov2018qtopt]. Deep reinforcement learning (training a network by trial, error, and a numerical reward) in simulation produced controllers for a legged robot and a dexterous hand that transferred to hardware [@hwangbo2019learning; @akkaya2019solving]. With that step the data changed character. A robot that learns from its own attempts chooses the states it will see, so its training data depends on its own behavior.</p>\n<p>Imitation learning, which trains a policy to reproduce recorded demonstrations, made human demonstration a practical route to new manipulation skills, and two ideas shaped the policies now in use. Action chunking predicts a short sequence of future actions from each observation, a design its authors motivated partly by the way a cloned policy's errors compound over time [@zhao2023learning]. Diffusion policies generate action sequences by iterative denoising, which lets one policy represent the several different ways a demonstrator might complete the same task [@chi2024diffusionpolicy]. The learned output had become a short plan of future motion, executed by controllers beneath it.</p>\n<p>The latest step changed where learned proposals come from. In digital software systems, foundation models matured as agentic reasoning engines capable of decomposing abstract goals, generating plans, and invoking external tools. Bringing that agentic reasoning into physical embodiment required grounding multimodal representations in spatial geometry, continuous dynamics, and motor coordination. Large multitask datasets collected on many robots prepared the ground [@brohan2023rt1; @open_x_embodiment_collaboration2024open], and between 2023 and 2025 several groups showed that a model pretrained on web-scale images and text can be fine-tuned to emit robot actions directly. These <strong>vision-language-action (VLA) models</strong> share one mechanism. A pretrained vision-language backbone encodes the camera images and an instruction, and an action head converts the backbone's output into motor commands. The head either decodes actions as discrete tokens, the way a language model decodes words [@brohan2023rt2; @kim2024openvla], or produces a continuous <em>action chunk</em>, a short sequence of future setpoints from one inference [@black2024pi0]. A backbone with billions of parameters cannot run at a controller's rate, so recent designs split the work across rates. A slow model interprets the scene, and a fast action model or on-robot decoder emits chunks that controllers execute [@bjorck2025gr00t; @geminirobotics2025]. These systems are dated instances of one step, in which a pretrained general-purpose backbone, rather than a policy trained for one task on one robot, proposes the motion. The sizes and rates their authors report are collected later in @tbl-03-state-of-practice. The running machine adopts the same hierarchy with two proposers: an <em>intent model</em>, the slow vision-language model that reads the scene and chooses the goal, and a <em>chunk policy</em>, the fast learned policy that emits action chunks toward that goal. Their rates are fixed on its machine card (@sec-boundary-running-machine). Because a general-purpose learned model can now propose motion for a general-purpose machine, deciding which component may <em>permit</em> that motion becomes a design question for every machine that adopts one.</p>\n<p>↪ The memory-bound cost of splitting the work between a slow intent model and a fast chunk policy on the running machine is developed in @sec-brain-two-proposers.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ TRAINING COMPUTE: ROBOT POLICIES VS. LANGUAGE MODELS (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-01-compute-by-domain and the paragraph after it.\n# │ Goal: Show how far the compute behind robot action models trails the\n# │       language frontier of the same year.\n# │ Show: Gap between two open robot policies and the language maximum of\n# │       their publication year.\n# │ How:  data/epoch_models_by_domain.csv (Epoch estimates).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int\nfrom binder.tools.figures import style as book_style\n\nclass ComputeByDomain:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants) ──────────────────────────────────────────────\n    _rows = book_style.load_series(&quot;vol4/01_boundary/data/epoch_models_by_domain.csv&quot;).entries\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (The Compute) ────────────────────────────────────────\n    _vla_c = float(next(r[&quot;training_flop&quot;] for r in _rows if r[&quot;model&quot;] == &quot;OpenVLA&quot;))\n    _gr00t_c = float(next(r[&quot;training_flop&quot;] for r in _rows if r[&quot;model&quot;] == &quot;GR00T N1 2B&quot;))\n    _lang_2024 = max(\n        float(r[&quot;training_flop&quot;])\n        for r in _rows\n        if r[&quot;domain_group&quot;] == &quot;Language&quot; and str(r[&quot;publication_date&quot;]).startswith(&quot;2024-&quot;) and r[&quot;training_flop&quot;] is not None\n    )\n    _lang_2025 = max(\n        float(r[&quot;training_flop&quot;])\n        for r in _rows\n        if r[&quot;domain_group&quot;] == &quot;Language&quot; and str(r[&quot;publication_date&quot;]).startswith(&quot;2025-&quot;) and r[&quot;training_flop&quot;] is not None\n    )\n    gap_vla = _lang_2024 / _vla_c\n    gap_gr00t = _lang_2025 / _gr00t_c\n    n_robotics = sum(1 for r in _rows if r[&quot;domain_group&quot;] == &quot;Robotics&quot; and r[&quot;training_flop&quot;] is not None)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(gap_vla &gt; 100 and gap_gr00t &gt; 1000, &quot;Prose: hundreds of times in 2024, thousands of times in 2025&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    gap_vla_str = fmt_int(round(gap_vla, -1))\n    gap_gr00t_str = fmt_int(round(gap_gr00t, -2))\n    n_robotics_str = fmt_int(n_robotics)\n]```\n\n:::: {#fig-01-compute-by-domain fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Robot models train on a sliver of frontier compute**: Estimated training compute, on a logarithmic axis, of the models the Epoch AI database tags as language models (gray) and as robotics models (green), from 2018. Robotics models that generate video of the world rather than actions are drawn as open squares and labeled by family. The open robot action policies sit orders of magnitude below the largest language models of the same year; on this record, the scaling that drove language models has not been applied to learned action. Compute values are Epoch&#x27;s estimates [@epochai2026models].&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Scatter plot of training compute from about 10 to the 16 to 10 to the 27 FLOP on a logarithmic axis against publication date from 2018 to 2026. Hundreds of small gray points for language models rise steadily to about 10 to the 27. About twenty green points for robotics models sit mostly between 10 to the 19 and 10 to the 23, with labeled action policies such as OpenVLA and GR00T N1, and a cluster of open green squares labeled as video world models near 10 to the 24 in early 2025.&quot;}\n\n\n\n::::\n\nThe scale of that transfer from web-scale pretraining is easy to overstate. In the Epoch AI database, the open robot action policies trained on hundreds to thousands of times less compute than the largest language models of their year (@fig-01-compute-by-domain) [@epochai2026models]. The gap is about `[ComputeByDomain.gap_vla_str]` times less for a 2024 vision-language-action model and about `[ComputeByDomain.gap_gr00t_str]` times less for a 2025 open robot foundation model, and only `[ComputeByDomain.n_robotics_str]` robotics models in the database carry a compute estimate at all. Physical AI is therefore not a smaller copy of the language-model scaling story. Its learned proposers are bounded by what can be collected from machines acting in the world and by what can run on the machine itself, two constraints that Parts I and II of this book treat as first-class.\n\nThe name *physical AI* is recent, and industry usage since 2024 applies it broadly to almost any AI that perceives and acts in the physical world [@nvidia2026physicalai]. That history runs through two research traditions that cover much of the same ground. **Embodied AI** descends from Brooks&#x27;s argument that intelligent behavior arises from a body situated in and interacting with its environment rather than from abstract representation alone [@brooks1991intelligence]. **Cyber-physical systems** name the integration of computation and networking with physical processes [@lee2008cyber], and they carry the timing and verification discipline of embedded engineering [@leeseshia2017embedded]. Embodied AI supplies much of the learning this book uses, and cyber-physical systems supply much of its timing and verification practice. The book&#x27;s subject is where the two meet at an actuator, and the definition developed next is deliberately narrower than industry usage, narrow enough that the scope test in @sec-boundary-scope-test can apply it to a given machine.\n\nRead as one story, this history moves the learned model step by step toward the actuator, as the band beneath the time axis of @fig-01-evolution records. At first nothing was learned. Then learned percepts fed hand-written planners, learned policies emitted motor commands, and finally pretrained general-purpose models began to propose motion for many kinds of machine. At each step, a guarantee that separation or human inspection used to supply had to move inside the machine. The familiar concerns of machine learning systems engineering come along, and each acquires physical stakes. Data stops being a fixed corpus because the machine&#x27;s own actions choose what it will observe next. Compute stops being a matter of throughput and cost alone because a late answer is paid for in stopping distance. Evaluation stops being a score on held-out examples because the competence that matters appears only in the closed loop. Deployment stops being a routine version rollout because a release grants a machine authority over motion near people. When a learned output becomes an irreversible action, none of these concerns can be settled inside the model. They belong to the whole machine, and they meet at the boundary where its commands become physical work.\n\n## The Question This Book Answers {#sec-boundary-central-question}\n\nThat convergence is why the problem now faces the whole field rather than a few robotics specialists. The central question of this book is why a better prediction can still produce a worse physical action, and what the rest of the machine must supply so that a learned proposal becomes useful work within justified limits. The book&#x27;s answer is an argument in four linked steps. The body&#x27;s physical limits bound what any proposal may ask for. The learned proposer&#x27;s competence holds only over the states that its data and closed-loop evaluation covered. For both reasons, an independent permission path, rather than the model itself, must decide what may execute at each tick. The evidence gathered about the whole machine then bounds the operating conditions under which that permission is granted. The argument first requires a shared vocabulary. That vocabulary covers the causal boundary and the cost of delay at it, the scope of physical AI, a five-level model of the machine that separates proposal from permission, and the four laws that organize the rest of the book.\n\nOne machine carries the argument through every chapter. A warehouse mobile manipulator must collect an object from a conveyor, open a spring-latched cage door, and carry its load through an aisle shared with people. These tasks join abilities often studied separately. The machine must recognize an unfamiliar object, choose a grasp, coordinate its arm and base, and respond when contact or the scene differs from expectation. Learning makes varied behavior possible; engineering must turn that behavior into useful, repeatable physical work.\n\nThis book develops the ability to design, teach, integrate, evaluate, and maintain such a machine within justified operating limits. Its recurring warehouse machine is an illustrative engineering design, not a report of a qualified deployment. The same task connects physical budgets, learning experiments, runtime decisions, and evidence for service. When the evidence is incomplete, the engineer must identify what measurement, new experience, or design change would support the next capability.\n\nConsider the base approaching the end of a storage rack while its model processes a camera image. A worker steps into the aisle before the next proposal returns. The computer can still discard that proposal, but the robot has continued moving throughout the calculation, so choosing an action requires reasoning about both the prediction and the physical state in which it will execute. Three things must therefore hold together: the model must recognize a useful action, the execution mechanism must judge it while the observation remains relevant, and the body must be able to deliver the admitted motion or a feasible alternative. A stronger prediction alone cannot recover clearance already consumed during computation, which is why the engineering problem leads from physical limits to the architecture that checks each proposed action.\n\nBuilding such a machine requires contributions from four disciplines that normally work apart. Machine learning supplies perception and action proposals. Computer systems determine how quickly those proposals compute and move across memory [@williams2009roofline]. Mechanics and control establish which motions the plant can physically sustain. Embedded systems enforce the real-time scheduling and bus rules that connect a proposal to an actuator. All four responsibilities meet at a single runtime question: whether the proposed action may execute under the machine&#x27;s current conditions. This book develops the reasoning needed to make and justify that decision.\n\n{{&lt; pagebreak &gt;}}\n\n::: {#dfn-boundary-physical-artificial-intelligence-physical-ai .callout-definition title=&quot;Physical artificial intelligence&quot;}\n\n***Physical artificial intelligence*** is the engineering of closed-loop machines in which learned models influence, without synchronous human approval, physical actions that change subsequent observations.\n\n1.  **Significance**: Model performance becomes a property of an operating machine. Its actions must fit the plant&#x27;s physical limits and the time available to respond.\n2.  **Distinction**: The scope test later in this chapter combines a learned model, consequential physical feedback, and delegated authority. Neither a model used only for advice nor an automated machine without a learned component meets all three criteria.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Treating the body as a peripheral device for a model. A useful proposal still needs an independent permission decision based on the current state, physical limits, and available fallback.\n\n:::\n\nThe argument starts where the definition does, with the difference between a proposed action and its physical effect. A short stopping calculation then puts a number on what delay costs at the causal boundary. A documented collision later in the chapter shows these responsibilities failing together, and four laws then distill them.\n\n## Defining the Causal Boundary {#sec-boundary-causal-boundary}\n\nThat difference between a proposed action and its physical effect separates advisory computing from physical AI. An advisory model that renders an inference on a dashboard or returns a JSON payload across a network has no direct electrical or mechanical coupling to physical actuators: an operator or downstream software filter can inspect, validate, or discard its output before any physical energy is expended. Digital errors are not benign (an operator who acts on flawed guidance can cause serious damage), but advisory software cannot directly alter physical state. Once an automated system acquires the delegated authority of definition \\ref{dfn-boundary-physical-artificial-intelligence-physical-ai} to turn a computed proposal directly into physical motion, safety arbitration cannot remain an external afterthought. It must live inside the machine architecture, at the causal boundary between digital intent and physical consequence.\n\n### From a proposed action to motion {#sec-boundary-tensors-to-silicon}\n\nIn modern computational frameworks, executing `action_chunk = policy(observation)` produces a floating-point tensor. That tensor represents an uncommitted proposal; converting it into physical motion requires an entire chain of intermediate controllers, bus interfaces, and power electronics. The causal boundary is the exact interface through which an admitted digital command begins driving physical change in the plant.\n\nA learned policy typically proposes a trajectory of future joint positions, target velocities, or motor torques. Those numerical arrays remain purely informational descriptions until a low-level controller validates, accepts, and begins executing them. The internal representation and predictive horizon of such action sequences are developed in @sec-brain-action-chunking; here the systems distinction is between proposing an action and authorizing its irreversible consequences.\n\nThat authorization is governed by the permission path, an independent mechanism defined in @sec-boundary-machine-model that alone holds the authority to pass commands to motor drives. The permission path verifies whether a proposed trajectory satisfies physical kinematic constraints, obeys velocity and acceleration bounds, and can complete safely within current time allowances. Once admitted, trajectory setpoints feed into dedicated motor controllers. A position loop compares each setpoint with the measured joint position and computes a correction, and a current controller sets the motor current that produces the torque the correction needs.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The bus timing and jitter bounds on command transport from the permission path to the drives are developed in @sec-nervous-deterministic-fieldbuses.\n:::\n\nConsider an electric motor driven by pulse-width modulation. A software write updates a compare register in a microcontroller timer, which sets how long each power switch stays on in every switching period. The resulting on-off pattern drives the inverter&#x27;s transistor switches, which turn the DC supply into current in the stator windings (the stationary coils). That current sets up a magnetic field that pulls on the magnets of the rotor (the spinning part) to produce torque, which accelerates the rotor shaft, transmission gears, and mechanical payload. The progression is asymmetric. Rewriting a planned setpoint in memory requires negligible energy and can be undone before the next clock cycle, but modifying a command that has already energized a winding requires physical work performed against mechanical inertia over finite time. A subsequent command can apply brakes or reverse drive current, but that corrective maneuver requires its own physical distance and must obey strict actuator limits.\n\n::: {#dfn-boundary-causal-boundary .callout-definition title=&quot;Causal boundary&quot;}\n\n***Causal boundary*** is the interface through which a digital command produces physical forces, currents, or flows.\n\n1.  **Significance**: A proposal can be rejected before it changes the plant. Once executed, it can alter the environment, dissipate energy, and change subsequent observations.\n2.  **Distinction**: Restoring a command in memory does not restore the machine&#x27;s prior physical state. Recovery requires another physical action, with its own limits and consequences.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Assuming that canceling a software task stops the motion it initiated. The machine needs a feasible stopping or holding response even when its proposer stops computing.\n\n:::\n\nOffline supervised learning evaluates models against static datasets where predictions never influence future test inputs. An embodied robot cannot rely on this decoupling. When the mobile base steers its chassis, its onboard cameras immediately capture a different visual field. When its manipulator nudges a carton, the object&#x27;s spatial coordinates and contact forces change. When the vehicle cruises along an aisle, its closing distance to personnel shifts in real time. The closed-loop feedback introduced in @sec-boundary-prediction-to-action applies directly to the learned model, because an acting machine reshapes the very environment that produces its future training and inference inputs.\n\nIn any embodied system, the environment evolves through closed-loop dynamics: the subsequent physical state $\\PhysicalWorldState_{t+1}$ and incoming sensory observation $\\MultimodalObservation_{t+1}$ depend directly on the action $\\CognitivePolicyAction_{t}$ issued by the policy inference:\n$$\\PhysicalWorldState_{t+1} = \\DiscretePlantDynamics(\\PhysicalWorldState_{t}, \\CognitivePolicyAction_{t}), \\quad \\MultimodalObservation_{t+1} \\sim \\ProbabilityFunction(\\MultimodalObservation \\mid \\PhysicalWorldState_{t+1})$$\nHere $\\DiscretePlantDynamics$ represents the physical plant and environmental dynamics (formulated without process noise for this introductory overview), and $\\ProbabilityFunction$ denotes the observation model transforming underlying physical states into discrete sensor measurements. Because each state transition feeds the next observation, a flawed action proposal can steer the machine into configurations its training data never covered. In those out-of-distribution states the model&#x27;s outputs degrade further, and the machine departs faster from its nominal trajectory. Advisory output stops at an informational interface. In physical AI the loop closes through the permission path, the motor actuators, and the plant, so the model&#x27;s own actions irreversibly reshape the observations $\\MultimodalObservation_{t}$ it receives next (@fig-01-causal-loop). Imitation learning formalizes this compounding divergence as **covariate shift**. Whereas independent errors in open-loop prediction simply add up across the task horizon $\\BehaviorCloningHorizon$ (the number of control steps the task lasts), a single mistake in closed-loop execution can push the machine into an unfamiliar state that causes elevated error rates across every remaining step of the rollout, so the expected cost of mistakes can grow quadratically with the horizon, $\\CovariateDriftBound$, rather than linearly [@ross2011reduction]. @Sec-data-covariate-shift develops the mechanics of this endogenous covariate shift. Contractive feedback controllers (stabilizing low-level tracking loops that pull perturbed states back toward the reference trajectory) or well-damped passive mechanics can absorb minor trajectory errors, although verifying behavior over long horizons still requires closed-loop evaluation rather than static offline benchmark scoring.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Corrective aggregation and the intervention data that defend a policy against covariate shift are developed in @sec-data-intervention-recovery.\n:::\n\n: **Recovery Data (ALVINN)**: The neural network that steered Carnegie Mellon&#x27;s Navlab autonomous van directly from camera imagery confronted this challenge in early trials: because human test drivers kept the van centered in the driving lane, training logs contained no examples of recovering from the road shoulder, causing the system to veer off whenever it drifted off center. To synthesize recovery demonstrations without hazardous trials, @pomerleau1991efficient transformed recorded road images using synthetic lateral shifts and calculated corresponding steering corrections back toward the lane center. While synthetic shifts work for simple geometric offsets, a physical policy must still be trained over the states its own actions visit. \n\n::: {#fig-01-causal-loop fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**The closed causal loop**: Advisory output (top) stops at an informational interface. In physical AI (bottom), permitted model proposals reach hardware registers, where electrical current produces force and motion. Those actions alter the environment and subsequent sensor observations.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Flowchart contrasting advisory predictions with physical AI. The upper path ends at an informational interface; the lower path carries sensor observations to a proposal, the permission path, motor actuators, and a changing physical plant.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\n### The running machine {#sec-boundary-running-machine}\n\nTo trace how a proposed action crosses the causal boundary on the warehouse mobile manipulator of @sec-boundary-central-question, the book needs one set of numbers that every later chapter can share. With them, the stop @sec-body derives, the timing @sec-nervous budgets, and the evidence Part IV demands all describe the same robot. @Tbl-01-machine-card fixes the fifteen parameters of the machine that later derivations use most, each with the evidence label the reader guide defines (measured, derived, datasheet, or illustrative, or chosen for a design decision). The stopping budget that follows uses five of them, the drive limit, the credible deceleration, the clear distance, the pre-brake delay, and the chunk lease inside that delay. This chapter needs only those five and, in one pitfall, the in-loop takeover time. The other rows use ideas (the jerk-limited stop, the intent model&#x27;s prefill and decode, human takeover studies) that the section named in each row&#x27;s Basis column develops. They appear here so that every chapter reads the same values. Subsequent worked examples cite the values by parameter name. For complete hardware specifications, the machine data sheet in @sec-appendix-systems-machine-data-sheet catalogs every other input alongside its programmatic registry key.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE RUNNING MACHINE'S CARD (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @tbl-01-machine-card in @sec-boundary-running-machine.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Fix the fifteen parameters later chapters use most, with their</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          evidence categories, and state the premise the credible</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          deceleration rests on.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    368 kg; 1.5 and 1.3 m/s; 2.0 m/s^2 on a dry floor (mu &gt;= 0.204 with</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          all weight on the driven wheels, 0.250 at the load share the drive</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          motors allow; tip-over unchecked); 7.11 m/s^3; 1.10 m; 133.6 ms;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          1 ms tick; 60 ms lease; 25 ms step and 50 ms replan; 336 ms intent</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          latency at 2 Hz; 605 ms and 4.47 s takeovers.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     machine_card() reads each row from the registry through the same</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          helper that prints the full data sheet; the friction premise comes</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          from calc_required_friction. Nothing is retyped here.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.embodied.datasheet import data_sheet_lookup, machine_card from mlsysim.fmt import MarkdownStr, fmt, check</p>\n<p>class MachineCard: \"\"\"The running machine's most-used parameters, with their evidence categories.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>card = machine_card()  # category: per row, from the registry and its provenance sheet = data_sheet_lookup()  # the braking premise rows of the full sheet</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>mu_all = sheet[\"mu_req\"].value  # friction a_brake needs, all weight on the driven wheels mu_share = sheet[\"mu_req_rated\"].value  # friction it needs at the largest allowed load share cg_unknown = sheet[\"cg_height\"].value is None</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(len(card) == 15, f\"The machine card prints fifteen rows, got {len(card)}.\") check(abs(mu_all - 0.204) &lt; 5e-4, f\"Dry-floor premise is mu &gt;= 0.204, got {mu_all}.\") check(abs(mu_share - 0.250) &lt; 5e-4, f\"At the rated load share the premise is mu &gt;= 0.250, got {mu_share}.\") check(cg_unknown, \"The tip-over check stays open until the loaded center of gravity is measured.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>value_str = {r.key: MarkdownStr(r.value_str) for r in card} category_str = {r.key: MarkdownStr(r.category_str) for r in card} mu_all_str = fmt(mu_all, precision=3) mu_share_str = fmt(mu_share, precision=3) ]```</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Category</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Loaded mass $\\Mass$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"m_loaded\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"m_loaded\"]]</code></td><td>Base, arm, and a full tote rack; an upper bound (@sec-body-five-budgets)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drive limit $\\SpeedBound$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"v_drive\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"v_drive\"]]</code></td><td>Class profile of a warehouse base</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aisle speed $\\AisleTravelSpeed$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"v_aisle\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"v_aisle\"]]</code></td><td>Set below the dry-floor speed ceiling (@sec-enforcement-composite-budget)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Credible deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"a_brake\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"a_brake\"]]</code></td><td>Loaded, on a dry floor whose grip supplies a friction coefficient $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\ge$ <code>[MachineCard.mu_all_str]</code> if all the weight bears on the driven wheels, or <code>[MachineCard.mu_share_str]</code> at the share the drive motors allow; tip-over is unchecked until the center of gravity is measured (@sec-body-stopping-distance-prebrake)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stop jerk bound $\\JerkLimit$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"j_brake\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"j_brake\"]]</code></td><td>Stipulated for the jerk-limited stop, whose deceleration bound is $\\ProfileAccelerationBound = \\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ (@sec-planning-c2-stopping-suffix)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rack-end clear distance $\\ClearDistance$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"d_clear\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"d_clear\"]]</code></td><td>Clearance the stop must fit inside</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"tau_delay\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"tau_delay\"]]</code></td><td>Observation age, chunk lease, tick, bus cycle, and brake onset (@sec-perception-latency-waterfall); excludes the dispatch-to-admission interval, which the spare pays for (@sec-placement-dispatch-admission)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Permission tick $\\PermissionLoopPeriod$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"t_tick\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"t_tick\"]]</code></td><td>Period of the chosen permission-loop rate (@sec-nervous-time-constants-phase-lag)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chunk lease $\\ChunkLease$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"t_lease\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"t_lease\"]]</code></td><td>Longest an admitted chunk may drive without renewal (@sec-nervous-lease-sizing)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Setpoint step $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"t_step\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"t_step\"]]</code></td><td>Spacing of the chunk policy's setpoints (@sec-brain-action-chunking)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chunk replan period</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"t_period\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"t_period\"]]</code></td><td>A whole number of setpoint steps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intent-model latency</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"t_intent\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"t_intent\"]]</code></td><td>Prefill plus decode on the application processor (@sec-brain-memory-wall)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intent-model rate</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"f_intent\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"f_intent\"]]</code></td><td>Largest rate whose period covers the latency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In-loop takeover $\\TakeoverResponseTime$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"t_takeover_in\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"t_takeover_in\"]]</code></td><td>Slow-bound human reaction plus console video and handshake (@sec-intervention-reaction-drift)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Out-of-loop takeover $\\DisengagedTakeoverResponseTime$</td><td><code>[MachineCard.value_str[\"t_takeover_out\"]]</code></td><td><code>[MachineCard.category_str[\"t_takeover_out\"]]</code></td><td>Take-over time of the 90th-percentile study condition plus video and handshake</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>The running machine's card</strong>: The fifteen parameters of the warehouse mobile manipulator that later calculations use most. A derived value built on illustrative inputs is illustrative evidence. Values print from the MLSysIM registry when the book is built. {#tbl-01-machine-card tbl-colwidths=\"[24,12,12,52]\"}</p>\n<p>With these parameters fixed, every later design problem on this platform starts from the same physical foundation. It asks which margin a commanded action approaches and whether the machine can execute an assured protective response before that margin is exhausted. The first margin to price is stopping clearance, and four rows of the card, the drive limit, the credible deceleration, the clear distance, and the pre-brake delay, are enough to price it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Delay consumes stopping clearance</h3>\n<p>When the loaded warehouse base cruises down an aisle at speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$ and a worker steps out from behind a rack, stopping requires clearance across two distinct phases. The first is the distance the base travels before braking forces engage. The second is the distance it travels while mechanical braking brings its momentum to zero. Assuming steady initial velocity $\\SpeedAtDetection$, uniform deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ once brakes engage, and a stationary obstacle, the elementary stopping distance satisfies the kinematic budget: $$\\StoppingDistance = \\underbrace{\\SpeedAtDetection \\PrebrakeDelay}<em>{\\text{travel during response delay}} + \\underbrace{\\frac{\\SpeedAtDetection^2}{2 \\CredibleBrakingDeceleration}}</em>{\\text{mechanical dissipation}} \\le \\ClearDistance$$ {#eq-boundary-stopping-budget} Here $\\SpeedAtDetection$ denotes initial forward speed, $\\PrebrakeDelay$ represents total pre-brake response latency, $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ is the sustained braking deceleration, and $\\ClearDistance$ is the usable line-of-sight clearance. The pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$ spans the time from when an obstacle first appears in a sensor exposure to the physical onset of retarding force. Specifically, it sums the age of the sensor observation upon handoff and the unexpired lease duration of any currently executing motion chunk (the lease is the longest an admitted chunk may drive before the permission path must act again). It also adds one discrete permission-path evaluation tick (the period at which the permission path re-checks the motion it has admitted), one bus communication cycle (the time to carry a command to the motor drives), and mechanical brake onset latency.</p>\n<p>The lease belongs in the worst case even though the permission path re-checks every tick, because of what that check can see. Each tick tests the admitted chunk against limits the permission path already holds (the chunk's freshness, its lease, and the kinematic bounds, measured against the wheel and joint encoders). The permission path has no camera pipeline of its own. On the detection path this budget models, evidence that a worker has stepped out therefore reaches the brakes only through the learned perception path and a replacement proposal. If the proposer stalls or fails to answer the worker, the most recently admitted chunk keeps driving until its lease expires, and only then does the permission path command the stop. The term deliberately excludes one interval, the dispatch-to-admission interval (the time while the learned policy computes a replacement proposal and the permission path admits it). Its length depends on where the proposer and the permission path run and how they communicate, which are decisions this chapter has not yet made. @Sec-placement-dispatch-admission therefore allocates it, and the finished budget pays for it from the spare this elementary budget leaves.</p>\n<p>The budget has two terms that grow differently with speed: travel during the delay grows linearly ($\\SpeedAtDetection \\PrebrakeDelay$), and travel while shedding kinetic energy grows with the square of speed ($\\SpeedAtDetection^2 / (2 \\CredibleBrakingDeceleration)$). Equating them gives the crossover speed $\\StoppingCrossoverSpeed = 2 \\CredibleBrakingDeceleration \\PrebrakeDelay$. Below $\\StoppingCrossoverSpeed$, delay travel is the larger term; above it, braking travel dominates. Shortening sensing, computation, and signaling reduces the delay term, while lowering speed reduces both terms: halving speed halves the delay travel and quarters the braking travel. Which change saves more clearance depends on the reductions available, not on term dominance alone. Solving @eq-boundary-stopping-budget for $\\SpeedAtDetection$ gives the fastest speed whose stop fits inside a verified clear distance, a ceiling @sec-body-dynamic-velocity-ceilings derives with the allowances this elementary form omits.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE RUNNING MACHINE&#x27;S ELEMENTARY STOPPING BUDGET (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @nbk-boundary-stopping-budget in @sec-boundary-kinematic-roofline,\n# │          and the takeaways.\n# │ Goal:    Close the stopping inequality against the card&#x27;s clear distance,\n# │          open the pre-brake delay into its registry terms, and show the\n# │          crossover speed and a half-speed what-if.\n# │ Show:    133.6 ms = 51.6 + 60 + 1 + 1 + 20 ms; 200.4 + 562.5 = 762.9 mm\n# │          inside 1.10 m (337.1 mm spare); v* = 0.53 m/s; backbone 22 ms is\n# │          33 mm; at 0.75 m/s the stop is 240.8 mm. Guard: the finished\n# │          budget of @sec-enforcement-composite-budget exceeds D_clear at the\n# │          drive limit, which the prose states without printing it.\n# │ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseAisleStopAtDriveLimit and its half-speed variant\n# │          (StoppingBudgetModel); the delay terms are RM/AISLE fields of the\n# │          warehouse mobile manipulator. The guard reuses calc_c2_stop_suffix\n# │          and calc_scurve_stop_distance exactly as\n# │          @sec-enforcement-composite-budget does. No value is retyped.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.physics.robotics import calc_c2_stop_suffix, calc_scurve_stop_distance\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, fmt_acceleration, fmt_percent, fmt_multiple, fmt_display_math, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass StoppingBudget:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Elementary stopping budget of the loaded base at its drive limit (illustrative inputs).&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    STOP = Scenarios.WarehouseAisleStopAtDriveLimit\n    HALF = Scenarios.WarehouseAisleStopHalfSpeed\n    v = STOP.speed  # category: illustrative (drive limit)\n    v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen (aisle speed on the card)\n    a_brake = STOP.deceleration  # category: illustrative (credible deceleration, loaded, dry floor)\n    d_clear = STOP.clear_distance  # category: illustrative (rack-end clear distance)\n    t_age = AISLE.perception_age  # category: derived (mid-exposure to proposer dispatch)\n    t_backbone = AISLE.t_backbone  # category: illustrative (perception backbone, inside t_age)\n    t_lease = AISLE.t_lease  # category: chosen (chunk lease)\n    t_tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen (one permission tick)\n    t_bus = AISLE.t_bus  # category: illustrative (one bus cycle)\n    t_onset = AISLE.t_brake_onset  # category: illustrative (brake command to retarding force)\n    tau_delay = STOP.delay  # category: derived (sum of the five terms above)\n    v_half = HALF.speed  # category: illustrative (half the drive limit)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    budget = STOP.result\n    budget_half = HALF.result\n    tau_sum = (t_age + t_lease + t_tick + t_bus + t_onset).to(millisecond)\n    blind = budget.delay_travel.to(millimeter)\n    d_brake = budget.braking_travel.to(millimeter)\n    d_stop = budget.stop_distance.to(millimeter)\n    spare = budget.spare.to(millimeter)\n    blind_share = budget.delay_share_of_braking\n    brake_over_blind = budget.braking_over_delay\n    v_star = budget.crossover_speed.to(meter / second)\n    d_backbone = budget.travel_over(t_backbone).to(millimeter)\n    d_lease = budget.travel_over(t_lease).to(millimeter)\n    d_per_ms = budget.travel_over(1 * millisecond).to(millimeter)\n    blind_half = budget_half.delay_travel.to(millimeter)\n    brake_half = budget_half.braking_travel.to(millimeter)\n    d_stop_half = budget_half.stop_distance.to(millimeter)\n    # Guard only: the finished budget of @sec-enforcement-composite-budget at the drive limit\n    j_brake = calc_c2_stop_suffix(v, a_brake)[&quot;peak_jerk&quot;].to(meter / second**3)\n    d_finished = (calc_scurve_stop_distance(v, a_brake, j_brake) + blind + AISLE.delta_loc + AISLE.delta_margin + AISLE.eps_track).to(millimeter)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(tau_sum.magnitude - tau_delay.to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;tau_delay must equal its five registry terms.&quot;)\n    check(abs((v_half - v / 2).to(meter / second).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;The what-if is at half the drive limit.&quot;)\n    check(abs(tau_delay.to(millisecond).magnitude - 133.6) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;Canon tau_delay is 133.6 ms, got {tau_delay}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(d_stop.magnitude - 762.9) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;Canon elementary stop is 762.9 mm, got {d_stop}.&quot;)\n    check(spare.magnitude &gt; 0, &quot;The elementary budget must fit inside D_clear at the drive limit.&quot;)\n    check(v_star &lt; v, &quot;At the drive limit braking, not delay, must dominate.&quot;)\n    check(d_finished &gt; d_clear, &quot;Prose says the finished budget exceeds D_clear at the drive limit.&quot;)\n    check(v_aisle &lt; v, &quot;Prose says the aisle speed sits below the drive limit.&quot;)\n    # margin_prebrake_delay_composition_bar.svg fig-alt hand-types 29.6, 2, and 20 ms\n    check(abs((t_age - t_backbone).to(millisecond).magnitude - 29.6) &lt; 5e-3, &quot;fig-alt: rest of observation age is 29.6 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs((t_tick + t_bus).to(millisecond).magnitude - 2.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;fig-alt: tick plus bus is 2 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_onset.to(millisecond).magnitude - 20.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;fig-alt: brake onset is 20 ms.&quot;)\n    check(t_lease &gt; t_age, &quot;Margin caption says the lease costs more than the backbone; it is the largest term.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    a_brake_str = fmt_acceleration(a_brake, unit=meter / second**2)\n    d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    tau_delay_str = fmt_latency(tau_delay, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_age_str = fmt_latency(t_age, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_backbone_str = fmt_latency(t_backbone, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_lease_str = fmt_latency(t_lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_tick_str = fmt_latency(t_tick, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_bus_str = fmt_latency(t_bus, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_onset_str = fmt_latency(t_onset, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    spare_str = fmt_length(spare, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    blind_share_str = fmt_percent(blind_share, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    brake_over_blind_mult_str = fmt_multiple(brake_over_blind, precision=1)\n    d_backbone_str = fmt_length(d_backbone, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    d_lease_str = fmt_length(d_lease, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    d_per_ms_str = fmt_length(d_per_ms, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    v_half_str = fmt_velocity(v_half, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    d_stop_half_str = fmt_length(d_stop_half, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    blind_half_str = fmt_length(blind_half, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    brake_half_str = fmt_length(brake_half, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n\n    _v = fmt(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    _a = fmt(a_brake, unit=meter / second**2, precision=0)\n    _tau_s = fmt(tau_delay, unit=second, precision=4)\n    _blind = fmt(blind, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    _brake = fmt(d_brake, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    _stop = fmt(d_stop, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    _clear = fmt(d_clear, unit=millimeter, precision=0, commas=False)\n    _spare = fmt(spare, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    _vstar = fmt(v_star, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    tau_sum_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;\\tau_{{\\text{{delay}}}} = {fmt(t_age, unit=millisecond, precision=1)} + {fmt(t_lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0)} + {fmt(t_tick, unit=millisecond, precision=0)} + {fmt(t_bus, unit=millisecond, precision=0)} + {fmt(t_onset, unit=millisecond, precision=0)} = {fmt(tau_delay, unit=millisecond, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{ms}}&quot;\n    )\n    blind_math = fmt_display_math(rf&quot;v\\,\\tau_{{\\text{{delay}}}} = {_v}\\ \\text{{m/s}} \\times {_tau_s}\\ \\text{{s}} = {_blind}\\ \\text{{mm}}&quot;)\n    brake_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;\\frac{{v^2}}{{2 a_{{\\text{{brake}}}}}} = \\frac{{({_v}\\ \\text{{m/s}})^2}}{{2 \\times {_a}\\ \\text{{m/s}}^2}} = {_brake}\\ \\text{{mm}}&quot;\n    )\n    close_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;d_{{\\text{{stop}}}} = {_blind} + {_brake} = {_stop}\\ \\text{{mm}} \\le D_{{\\text{{clear}}}} = {_clear}\\ \\text{{mm}}, \\quad D_{{\\text{{clear}}}} - d_{{\\text{{stop}}}} = {_spare}\\ \\text{{mm}}&quot;\n    )\n    vstar_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;v^{{*}} = 2 a_{{\\text{{brake}}}} \\tau_{{\\text{{delay}}}} = 2 \\times {_a}\\ \\text{{m/s}}^2 \\times {_tau_s}\\ \\text{{s}} \\approx {_vstar}\\ \\text{{m/s}}&quot;\n    )\n]```\n\nEvery input to the budget comes from the running machine. Its speed, deceleration, clear distance, and pre-brake delay are rows of the machine card (@tbl-01-machine-card), and the five terms of that delay come from the same MLSysIM registry, so the notebook&#x27;s givens are the ones every later chapter refines.\n\n\n\n::: {#nbk-boundary-stopping-budget .callout-notebook title=&quot;Does the base stop inside its clearance?&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: The loaded base cruises toward a rack end at its drive limit, and a worker may step out at the end of the rack. The permission path must stop the base inside the rack-end clear distance on the machine card. Does the elementary stopping budget of @eq-boundary-stopping-budget fit, which term dominates, and how much of the delay belongs to the learned model?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* Speed $\\SpeedAtDetection =$ `[StoppingBudget.v_str]`, the drive limit (illustrative).\n* Credible deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration =$ `[StoppingBudget.a_brake_str]`, loaded on a dry floor (illustrative).\n* Clear distance $\\ClearDistance =$ `[StoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` (illustrative).\n* Pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay =$ `[StoppingBudget.tau_delay_str]` (derived). It is the sum of five registry terms. The first is the observation age of the camera frame that first shows the worker, `[StoppingBudget.t_age_str]`, which includes the `[StoppingBudget.t_backbone_str]` perception backbone (the rest is half the exposure, sensor readout, transfer, image processing, and the handoff to the proposer, itemized in @sec-perception-latency-waterfall). The second is the chunk lease of `[StoppingBudget.t_lease_str]`, the longest an admitted command may drive before the permission path must act. The remaining terms are one permission tick of `[StoppingBudget.t_tick_str]`, one bus cycle of `[StoppingBudget.t_bus_str]`, and a brake onset of `[StoppingBudget.t_onset_str]`. It excludes the dispatch-to-admission interval, for the reason given with @eq-boundary-stopping-budget.\n\n**Math**:\n\n`[StoppingBudget.tau_sum_math]`\n`[StoppingBudget.blind_math]`\n`[StoppingBudget.brake_math]`\n`[StoppingBudget.close_math]`\n`[StoppingBudget.vstar_math]`\n\n**Result**: The elementary budget fits, with `[StoppingBudget.spare_str]` to spare. The drive limit sits above the crossover speed, so braking dominates: it needs `[StoppingBudget.brake_over_blind_mult_str]` the travel of the delay, and the delay adds `[StoppingBudget.blind_share_str]` to the braking distance. At half speed, `[StoppingBudget.v_half_str]`, delay travel halves to `[StoppingBudget.blind_half_str]` while braking quarters to `[StoppingBudget.brake_half_str]`, for a stop of `[StoppingBudget.d_stop_half_str]`.\n\n**Systems insight**: The learned model&#x27;s backbone accounts for `[StoppingBudget.t_backbone_str]` of the delay, or `[StoppingBudget.d_backbone_str]` of travel; the chunk lease alone costs `[StoppingBudget.d_lease_str]`. Each added millisecond of delay anywhere in the chain costs `[StoppingBudget.d_per_ms_str]` at this speed, so a slower but more accurate model is a distance cost the budget must absorb. The spare is not yet a safety margin. @Sec-enforcement-composite-budget adds the localization bound, a protective margin, tracking error, and the longer stop of a jerk-limited brake (one that limits jerk, the rate of change of deceleration, by ramping its braking up instead of stepping it). Together they exceed the clear distance at the drive limit, which is why the card sets the aisle speed at `[StoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]`.\n\n:::\n\n::: {#fig-01-kinematic-roofline fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Delay travel and braking travel against speed**: The elementary stopping budget of the loaded base, split into travel during the `[StoppingBudget.tau_delay_str]` pre-brake delay, which grows linearly with speed, and braking travel at `[StoppingBudget.a_brake_str]`, which grows with its square. The two terms are equal at the crossover speed $\\StoppingCrossoverSpeed$; above it braking dominates. At the `[StoppingBudget.v_str]` drive limit the elementary stop fits inside the `[StoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` clear distance, before the allowances that later chapters add.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Plot of distance in millimeters against speed from 0 to 2 meters per second. A straight line for travel during the delay and a rising parabola for braking travel cross at a marked crossover speed near 0.5 meters per second. Their sum rises more steeply and meets a dashed horizontal line marking the clear distance. A marked point at the drive limit of 1.5 meters per second sits on the sum curve below the line.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ DELAY TRAVEL AND BRAKING TRAVEL AGAINST SPEED (FIGURE)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @fig-01-kinematic-roofline in @sec-boundary-kinematic-roofline</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    show the two terms of the elementary budget crossing at v*, and the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          drive-limit stop against the clear distance</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    v tau_delay (133.6 ms), v^2/(2 a_brake) (2.0 m/s^2), their sum,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          D_clear 1.10 m, v* = 0.53 m/s, the 1.5 m/s point at 762.9 mm</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     StoppingBudget inputs over a speed grid; no value is retyped</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import numpy as np from mlsysim import viz from mlsysim.fmt import check</p>\n<p>SB = StoppingBudget MPS = meter / second</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (from boundary-stopping-budget) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>tau = SB.tau_delay.to(second).magnitude a = SB.a_brake.to(MPS / second).magnitude d_clear = SB.d_clear.to(millimeter).magnitude v_op = SB.v.to(MPS).magnitude v_star = SB.v_star.to(MPS).magnitude d_op = SB.d_stop.to(millimeter).magnitude</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v = np.linspace(0.0, 2.0, 401) delay = v <em> tau </em> 1000.0 braking = v*<em>2 / (2 </em> a) * 1000.0 total = delay + braking</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(v_op <em> tau </em> 1000.0 + v_op*<em>2 / (2 </em> a) <em> 1000.0 - d_op) &lt; 1e-6, \"The plotted point must equal the notebook's stop.\") check(abs(v_star </em> tau - v_star*<em>2 / (2 </em> a)) &lt; 1e-9, \"The two terms must be equal at v*.\") check(d_op &lt; d_clear, \"The drive-limit point must sit below the clear distance.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. PLOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>fig, ax, COLORS, plt = viz.setup_plot(figsize=(6.2, 4.2)) ax.plot(v, delay, color=COLORS[\"BrownLine\"], lw=1.8, label=r\"travel during the delay, $v\\,\\tau_{\\mathrm{delay}}$\") ax.plot(v, braking, color=COLORS[\"BlueLine\"], lw=1.8, label=r\"braking travel, $v^2/(2a_{\\mathrm{brake}})$\") ax.plot(v, total, color=COLORS[\"primary\"], lw=2.0, label=r\"elementary stop, $d_{\\mathrm{stop}}$\") ax.axhline(d_clear, color=COLORS[\"RedLine\"], lw=1.3, ls=(0, (6, 3))) ax.text(0.04, d_clear + 30, f\"clear distance {d_clear / 1000:.2f} m\", color=COLORS[\"RedLine\"], fontsize=9, va=\"bottom\") ax.plot([v_star], [v_star <em> tau </em> 1000.0], \"o\", ms=6, mfc=\"white\", mec=COLORS[\"primary\"], mew=1.4, zorder=5) ax.annotate( f\"$v^<em>$ = {v_star:.2f} m/s\", xy=(v_star, v_star </em> tau * 1000.0), xytext=(v_star + 0.12, 25), fontsize=9, arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=COLORS[\"primary\"], lw=0.8), ) ax.plot([v_op], [d_op], \"o\", ms=7, color=COLORS[\"primary\"], zorder=5) ax.annotate( f\"drive limit {v_op:.1f} m/s: {d_op:.1f} mm\", xy=(v_op, d_op), xytext=(v_op - 0.62, d_op + 170), fontsize=9, arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=COLORS[\"primary\"], lw=0.8), bbox=dict(boxstyle=\"square,pad=0.1\", fc=\"white\", ec=\"none\"), ) ax.set_xlim(0, 2.0) ax.set_ylim(0, 1600) ax.set_xlabel(\"Initial speed, $v$ (m/s)\") ax.set_ylabel(\"Distance (mm)\") ax.legend(loc=\"upper left\", bbox_to_anchor=(0.0, 0.93), fontsize=9, frameon=False) plt.tight_layout() plt.show() ]```</p>\n<p>The running machine's drive limit lies well above the crossover speed the notebook computed (@fig-01-kinematic-roofline), so braking, not delay, sets most of its stop, and the sum still clears the dashed clear-distance line before any later allowance is added. These values are representative parameters and a derived latency profile, not measured stopping distances for a real chassis, but they already show the two levers a designer holds. Shortening response latency contracts the linear term, whereas lowering cruising speed contracts both, the braking term quadratically. Selecting a model purely on offline accuracy overlooks the first lever, because added inference latency is added travel.</p>\n<p>The budget just computed models one particular detection path, and naming it shows which learned computation it charges. The worker is seen by the learned perception stack, so the perception backbone sits inside the observation age. The chunk policy's own compute time does not appear, because the lease, not the policy, bounds how long an admitted chunk may keep driving once the proposer falls silent. A machine can also carry a second, independent protective path with its own sensing, timing, power, and fallback logic, such as a certified safety scanner wired to the drives. A stop that path triggers bypasses perception dispatch and the lease entirely. The backbone and lease terms leave its delay, and its own sensing and switching latency take their place, while the braking travel and the allowances stay. Under that decoupled architecture, the protective path's latency, rather than the execution time of the neural network, governs the protective stop. Industrial automated guided vehicles (AGVs) implement this separation today, with safety standards mandating certified safety scanners that halt the machine before human contact, independent of fleet management or guidance computers [@iso3691part4]. This book layers learned sensorimotor proposers atop deterministic safety kernels and analyzes the architectural contracts required between them.</p>\n<p>↪ The scanner path's own timing budget, and how the software stopping budget maps onto certified safety functions, are developed in @sec-enforcement-certified-functions.</p>\n<p>Deploying this budget in real-world facilities requires several additional physical allowances. Beyond the allowances the notebook's systems insight names (localization, a protective margin, tracking error, and the longer jerk-limited stop), real-world controllers must also account for unmodeled initial acceleration and degraded tire-floor traction. Dynamic obstacles complicate the clearance budget even further: if a pedestrian walks toward the mobile robot at speed $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$, the relative closing distance contracts during both the pre-brake delay and the mechanical deceleration phase. For this reason, functional safety standards mandate adding a pedestrian approach-speed allowance to the vehicle's own braking distance [@iso13855]. Later chapters formalize these dynamic constraints and allocate delay budgets across compute hardware. Even before they do, a learned policy's behavior is acceptable only inside the real-time envelope that plant momentum sets.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h3\">Irreversibility and recovery</h3>\n<p>The stopping budget prices one consequence of irreversibility. The other is what recovery becomes. A database transaction that aborts restores the state it started from, but a gripper that has permanently deformed a workholding fixture, or a winding whose insulation has degraded from overheating, has no earlier state to return to. Recovery on a physical machine is therefore a new trajectory, not a reversal.</p>\n<p>Corrective commands can brake the motors, release a grasp, or plan a diversion, but each is itself motion, limited by the torque the actuators can deliver, by friction, and by the speeds and accelerations the drives can reach. Which limit governs depends on what the machine is doing. For the mobile base it is stopping clearance under momentum. When the arm closes its jaws on a fragile component or braces against an assembly fixture, it is contact force, the flex of the gears and belts between motor and gripper (transmission compliance), and heat in the windings. Because recovery is constrained motion, the permission path must check before it admits an action that a feasible recovery from the resulting state will still exist; checking afterward is too late.</p>\n<p>↪ The contact loads a transmission delivers to a gripped part or a latch, the arm's limiting quantity here, are developed in @sec-body-transmission-compliance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h2\">Three Machine Classes</h2>\n<p>As the warehouse mobile manipulator travels down an aisle, its primary engineering challenge is kinetic: preserving enough clearance to stop safely before an obstacle. When the same robot presses against a door latch, the governing constraint shifts to contact mechanics, regulating interaction forces to avoid damaging the mechanism or the workpiece. When holding a suspended payload stationary, the machine performs zero mechanical work, yet its actuators must sustain continuous stall torque without exhausting thermal headroom or browning out the shared electrical supply. Because each phase of the task spends a different physical margin, the book names three machine classes by the margin each one spends (@fig-01-three-archetypes).</p>\n<p>The first class, <strong>mass-dominated mobility</strong> (Class 1), spends stopping clearance. A mobile base, delivery vehicle, or aerial drone carries significant momentum and cannot halt instantaneously. Forward velocity, control and sensing latency, and available braking deceleration or reverse thrust jointly dictate the spatial clearance required to arrest or redirect its motion.</p>\n<p>The second class, <strong>contact-dominated manipulation</strong> (Class 2), spends force and deformation margin. When an articulated arm presses against a stiff fixture, minute position displacements can generate destructive contact forces over fractions of a millimeter. Physical contact is both the means of task success (seating a part or opening a latch requires deliberate force) and the immediate hazard. The engineering task is to supply the wrenches (the combined forces and torques) needed to complete the assembly while guaranteeing that contact forces never exceed the mechanical yield limits of the payload or the manipulator.</p>\n<p>The third class, <strong>energy- and thermal-dominated operation</strong> (Class 3), spends heat and electrical supply margins. Supporting a heavy load or resisting gravity demands sustained motor current, driving continuous Joule heating in actuator windings even when joints remain stationary. Concurrent peak current draws from drive inverters, compute accelerators, and sensor payloads can induce voltage sag across a shared DC bus. The system must sustain these thermal and electrical loads across the entire operational profile while reserving sufficient energy and thermal headroom to execute a safe protective abort.</p>\n<p>These three classes identify dominant physical constraints rather than mutually exclusive hardware categories. A practical robot transitions across all three regimes throughout a single task. The running machine integrates a wheeled base, an articulated arm, navigation lidar, and wrist cameras. An end-to-end learned policy can coordinate simultaneous base trajectories and arm proposals [@fu2024mobile], but the integrated machine must enforce the safety envelopes of every regime concurrently. A fixed-base arm still faces thermal and bus-voltage limits during prolonged holding, just as a wheeled base encounters contact constraints when docking against a workstation.</p>\n<p>A humanoid robot exposes the tight coupling across all three regimes. Catching a shifted load perturbs dynamic balance, requiring an immediate recovery step that demands peak joint torque. That torque surge draws heavy current from the shared DC bus and injects rapid thermal energy into the joint windings. If the power distribution network sags or thermal protection limits current, the recovery step fails and the machine falls. Shielding the onboard computing stack from reset does not by itself supply the transient mechanical energy needed to stabilize the body. The part-ending humanoid examples throughout this book test how each systems principle survives these tightly coupled physical demands.</p>\n<p>↪ The multi-contact balance mechanics a humanoid's recovery step depends on are developed in @sec-appendix-control-centroidal.</p>\n<p>Identifying whether a task spends stopping clearance, contact force, or thermal headroom tells the engineer which physical budget binds, regardless of how the controller is implemented. Whether the machine also requires the proposal-and-permission architecture of physical AI, rather than a classical feedback loop or an offline tool, depends on a separate test of how its decisions are generated and authorized.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Physical AI Scope Test</h2>\n<p>A robotic arm in a fulfillment center can follow a fixed motion profile under a proportional-integral-derivative (PID) controller (a classical hand-tuned feedback law that corrects in proportion to the present error, its accumulated sum, and its rate of change). It can also execute an offline trajectory pre-screened and loaded by a human technician, or stream joint torques directly from a learned policy running on real-time sensor frames. While all three regimes actuate identical electric motors and gear trains, they impose different architectural obligations on the computing system. The scope test establishes the operational boundaries that define physical AI throughout this book.</p>\n<p>The scope of physical AI is defined by the simultaneous intersection of three structural criteria:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Learned Statistical Model:</strong> The system relies on a policy, perception encoder, or world model whose runtime behavior emerges from training data rather than an inspectable closed-form specification, as seen in deep neural networks or diffusion policies [@chi2024diffusionpolicy]. Correctness cannot be formally proven across all state configurations from source code alone; engineers must establish operational validity empirically across a defined operating domain. By contrast, a linear plant model fitted through classical system identification, where parameter uncertainty yields provable stability margins, falls outside this category. A high-capacity neural policy falls within it.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequential Physical Feedback:</strong> The system's actions alter the material state of the surrounding physical world, and those state changes directly govern what its sensors measure in subsequent time steps under classical mechanics, thermodynamics, or electromagnetism. The environment cannot be paused or rolled back, and energy dissipated into it cannot be recalled.</li>\n<li><strong>Delegated Authority:</strong> The system's outputs reach execution without synchronous human approval for each control cycle. In a machine that also meets the second criterion, that execution is actuation, so the authority is physical. A learned component holds this authority whenever its output (a raw percept, a semantic state estimate, or a proposed motion trajectory) can propagate to live actuators without such approval. The learned network need not write directly to motor driver registers; the system possesses delegated authority whenever learned signals actively shape downstream actuation commands. Adding downstream safety checks or deterministic rate limiters does not vacate this authority, provided accepted proposals still drive hardware motion.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Together, these three criteria delineate the domain of physical AI. The three-set Venn diagram in @fig-01-scope-venn formalizes this boundary, locating physical AI at the central intersection where a learned model, consequential physical feedback, and delegated authority converge. Each pairwise overlap holds systems that meet only two of the three criteria. Advisory systems place a learned model inside a physical process, as when a technician inspects learned toolpaths before loading them onto a mill, but a person still approves each action. Digital autonomy executes learned outputs without human approval, as in an automated trading engine, but its actions land in software rather than in physics. Classical control closes a physical feedback loop automatically, as in a PID speed governor, but relies on an analytical control law rather than a learned model. Excluding these pairwise overlaps restricts the design problem to systems where statistical uncertainty collides directly with physical irreversibility.</p>\n<p>: <strong>System Identification</strong>: The control-engineering practice of fitting low-order dynamic models to measured input-output time series. When this fitting establishes bounded parameter uncertainty under stated modeling assumptions, control engineers can use those bounds to prove stability guarantees before deploying the controller. Modern high-dimensional neural networks lack tractable closed-form uncertainty bounds and therefore require empirical verification across explicitly specified operating domains.</p>\n<p>The first pairwise exclusion shows why a learned model alone does not make a machine a physical AI system. Consider an offline trajectory generator that optimizes smooth paths using a learned diffusion model. If a manufacturing engineer must inspect, validate, and load those toolpaths onto a CNC mill prior to cutting metal, the statistical model operates only as an advisory tool. The human retains ultimate physical authority. Similarly, a vision model that highlights defects on an assembly line display assists human inspection without closing the physical loop.</p>\n<p>Conversely, delegated authority does not require end-to-end neural control. Most production autonomous systems adopt modular architectures where deep neural networks handle perception while deterministic trajectory planners compute actual actuator setpoints. These systems satisfy the delegated-authority test through perception: if an unvetted object detection or bounding-box estimate triggers an automated swerve or emergency braking maneuver, the learned representation directly dictates plant dynamics. The autonomous test vehicle in the Tempe collision examined in @sec-boundary-when-fails is such a machine: changing classifications in its modular perception fed the path predictions that decided whether it would brake.</p>\n<p>Shared autonomy falls within physical AI whenever a learned sub-system modifies live motion before a human operator can intervene. In a robotic surgical console equipped with learned haptic virtual fixtures, for example, the assistive policy exerts real forces on the master tool manipulators to guide the surgeon around delicate anatomical structures. Even if the surgeon holds administrative responsibility for the procedure, high-bandwidth force cues alter physical tissue contact well within normal human neurological reaction latency (typically 150 ms to 250 ms). Because the machine enforces learned spatial guidance dynamically without requiring the surgeon to review and acknowledge each micro-correction, it exercises delegated authority over physical motion.</p>\n<p>When classifying an ambiguous machine, engineers must evaluate each of the three criteria independently. Implementing an independent deterministic veto, such as an analog brake relay triggered by a certified safety lidar, does not strip a machine of delegated authority, because learned proposals still govern all nominal motion up to the intervention threshold. In contrast, requiring a human operator to screen and authorize every trajectory command before transmission reliably removes that authority. Once a design satisfies all three criteria, it qualifies as physical AI, and its computing architecture must respect the hard timing and causal boundaries established in @sec-boundary-causal-boundary.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Delay vs. braking</strong>: Explain why response delay and active braking consume separate portions of stopping clearance, and identify which one dominates above the crossover speed.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Stopping budget estimate</strong>: Using the machine card, estimate how far the elementary stop at the <code>[StoppingBudget.v_str]</code> drive limit grows if the <code>[StoppingBudget.t_lease_str]</code> chunk lease doubles, and determine whether it still fits inside the <code>[StoppingBudget.d_clear_str]</code> clear distance.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Binding margins</strong>: Identify the relevant physical margin when the warehouse machine travels, presses a latch, and holds a load.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Scope criteria</strong>: Apply all three scope criteria to a new machine, including one whose learned output passes through an independent veto.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Satisfying the scope test binds statistical uncertainty directly to hardware consequences. To design systems that operate safely under these demands, computer systems engineers must map how timing, state estimation, and control authority distribute along the execution path from raw sensor frames to physical actuator motion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Machine in Five Levels</h2>\n<p>Mapping that execution path starts with what happens when the learned proposer falters in motion. When the running machine's chunk policy stalls mid-aisle, something other than the policy must still be able to stop the base. When the policy emits a chunk that would carry the arm through a coworker's hand, something other than the policy must refuse it. An embodied machine therefore needs an architecture that prevents learned models from writing directly to the actuators they could damage, while still letting those models guide physical behavior.</p>\n<p>The Body establishes what motion, force, and mechanical power the hardware can physically sustain. The Brain generates learned proposals intended to operate within those capabilities. Mediating between them, the Nervous System coordinates communication and control, preserving both the temporal margin and the hardware authority required to intercept and refuse an inadmissible command. Adding the interface where commands become current and the environment the machine acts on gives a five-level model that runs from learned computation down to the physical world:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>The Brain (Level 1):</strong> The learned models that interpret sensory observations and propose candidate goals, trajectories, or torques. Generating a proposed trajectory or sequence of setpoints confers no intrinsic authority to execute it.</li>\n<li><strong>The Nervous System (Level 2):</strong> The deterministic communication buses, real-time schedulers, and control loops that bridge proposals to physical execution. Its permission path evaluates, filters, or rejects proposals against fresh sensory evidence and invariant physical limits, and it executes a fallback shown feasible from the current state under the stated sensing, plant, and response-time assumptions when the proposer falters.</li>\n<li><strong>The Body (Level 3):</strong> The physical plant, including motor drives, transmissions, electromagnetic windings, structural linkages, and energy storage, that delivers mechanical action.</li>\n<li><strong>The Boundary (Level 4):</strong> The hard physical and electrical interface where digital registers translate into actuator voltages or currents, and where physical phenomena register across sensor transducers as raw measurements.</li>\n<li><strong>The World (Level 5):</strong> The external physical environment (surfaces, payloads, obstacles, ambient lighting, and human collaborators), which evolves continuously under the laws of physics regardless of whether the machine measures it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The list orders the levels by abstraction, from learned computation down to physics, not along the path a command travels. A command leaves the Nervous System, crosses the Boundary at a register write, and only then drives current through the Body's windings. The Boundary is listed after the Body because it gathers the transducers at both edges of the machine, the register writes that energize the windings and the sensor captures that turn light, force, and motion into numbers. Those are the places where the machine exchanges energy and information with the World. The actuation half of this level is the causal boundary defined earlier in the chapter. The proposal boundary that follows is not a level at all, but the line between the Brain and the Nervous System that separates proposing from permitting.</p>\n<p>To enforce that proposal boundary in hardware, a concrete physical AI implementation often maps the five-level hierarchy across distinct computational tiers. Learned proposers run on high-throughput accelerator hosts (such as an application processor paired with a GPU or neural processing unit). The permission path executes on an independent real-time microcontroller or dedicated real-time operating system (RTOS) core. The current loops that regulate motor-phase current run directly on dedicated motor drives. This architectural isolation is rooted in strict timing guarantees and fault-containment boundaries rather than specific silicon families or nominal operating system labels.</p>\n<p>↪ The loaded test of the two-processor split that realizes this isolation is developed in @sec-placement-testing-separation.</p>\n<p>Governance does not constitute a sixth level in this real-time control hierarchy. Instead, it serves as the operational and lifecycle envelope enclosing all five. Governance determines which entity holds valid command authority, what verification evidence licenses the machine to operate, and when that license lapses and must be renewed. Governance transitions unfold across the human and operational timescales of safety interventions, policy rollouts, and maintenance releases, rather than the millisecond rhythms of real-time control loops.</p>\n<p>↪ The authority state machine that hands each actuator channel to exactly one owner during a transfer is developed in @sec-intervention-authority-transitions.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Proposal boundary</strong></em> is the line between a learned model, which may only propose an action, and the <strong>permission path</strong>, an independent mechanism with bounded latency that the model cannot delay or corrupt and that alone may permit the action to reach an actuator. The permission path validates every proposal against fresh sensory evidence, the stopping envelope (the state space from which the machine can safely decelerate to a halt prior to obstacle collision), and a fallback trajectory shown feasible from the current state. On the running machine, the algorithmic admission checks remain an unrated performance layer (one not certified to a functional-safety rating) above separate certified protective functions. @Sec-enforcement-certified-functions explains that distinction. If any condition is violated, the permission path rejects the proposal within the established response bound.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Prevents learned model outputs from directly manipulating actuators. This architectural protection remains dependable only as long as the permission path's independent sensing, dynamic models, and fallback maneuvers remain physically valid.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Even a deep reinforcement learning policy trained to emit joint torques possesses zero raw actuator-write authority; its output remains a mere proposal until explicitly admitted by the permission path.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Treating the permission path as a cosmetic post-processing filter or output clamp, rather than an autonomous supervisory mechanism capable of seizing control when the learned proposer hangs, violates deadlines, emits pathological values, or falls silent.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The proposal boundary determines who holds the authority to approve an action, whereas the causal boundary marks the physical interface where an admitted command alters the state of the plant. The Nervous System bridges this divide, verifying proposals against hard constraints before energizing the drives.</p>\n<p>Readers steeped in classical control theory will recognize this design as a supervisory architecture in which the proposal boundary implements runtime assurance. In classic Simplex architectures, a complex, high-performance controller (here, the learned policy) is paired with a certified baseline controller, coordinated by decision logic that determines which subsystem drives the plant [@seto1998simplex; @sha2001using]. The lineage table in @sec-boundary-bedrock-laws (@tbl-01-law-lineage) maps this book's terms to their established names once the four laws are stated.</p>\n<p>↪ Runtime assurance for physical AI, which pairs the learned proposer with a resident protective controller and an output selector, is developed in @sec-enforcement-deterministic-gatekeeper.</p>\n<p>Hardware fault isolation alone does not guarantee safety, because architectural independence also constrains the permission path's sensor inputs. The learned detection path used in the stopping budget prices response delay, but its lease cannot protect against a perception error that produces timely, unsafe proposals. If an enforcement monitor validates a proposed trajectory using the very same learned perception pipeline that synthesized it, a single perception error will corrupt both components simultaneously. The sensing pipelines that grant execution authority must therefore maintain distinct failure modes from the learned models they supervise.</p>\n<p>Across the five stacked levels in @fig-01-locator, characteristic operating rates span four orders of magnitude. The continuous physical World sits at the bottom, followed by the event-timed Boundary, the 10–25 kHz current loops of the mechanical Body, and the 1 kHz permission loop of the Nervous System. At the top, the hierarchical Brain operates from 40 Hz setpoints to 2 Hz intent models. The prominent gap between Brain and Nervous System marks the foundational proposal boundary: components above this line may only formulate proposals, while only deterministic subsystems below it possess the authority to command physical actuators. The operating frequencies charted across the levels reflect the reference architecture analyzed throughout this book, rather than immutable physical constants for every robotic platform. Subsequent chapters derive these timing budgets directly from task dynamics and mechanical plant characteristics. What remains fundamental across all physical AI systems is the proposal boundary that severs learned computation from unchecked execution authority.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ LOCATOR RATES (LEGO GUARD FOR A HAND-DRAWN FIGURE)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-01-locator (fig01_machine_levels.svg) in\n# │          @sec-boundary-machine-model.\n# │ Goal:    Keep the rates drawn on the Brain and Nervous System bands equal to\n# │          the running machine&#x27;s registry values.\n# │ Show:    intent model 2 Hz; chunk renewal 20 Hz; setpoints 40 Hz; 1 kHz tick.\n# │ How:     Read the registry; check() fails the build if a registry change\n# │          leaves the SVG labels stale. Nothing prints.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check\n\nclass LocatorRates:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Registry rates that fig01_machine_levels.svg draws as text.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\n    f_intent = RM.intent_rate.to(hertz).magnitude  # category: chosen\n    f_chunk = RM.chunk_rate.to(hertz).magnitude  # category: chosen\n    f_step = (1 / RM.chunk_step).to(hertz).magnitude  # category: derived from the chosen step\n    f_tick = RM.permission_rate.to(hertz).magnitude  # category: chosen\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    drawn = {&quot;intent&quot;: 2.0, &quot;chunk&quot;: 20.0, &quot;step&quot;: 40.0, &quot;tick&quot;: 1000.0}  # labels in the SVG\n    check(abs(f_intent - drawn[&quot;intent&quot;]) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Locator draws intent at 2 Hz; registry says {f_intent}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(f_chunk - drawn[&quot;chunk&quot;]) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Locator draws chunk renewal at 20 Hz; registry says {f_chunk}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(f_step - drawn[&quot;step&quot;]) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Locator draws setpoints at 40 Hz; registry says {f_step}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(f_tick - drawn[&quot;tick&quot;]) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Locator draws a 1 kHz permission loop; registry says {f_tick}.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    # Guard-only class: it pins the values drawn in the figure; no prose outputs.\n]```\n\n::: {#fig-01-locator fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**The five-level machine**: Each band carries its pinned rate and the chapters that work on it. The wider gap between Brain and Nervous System marks the proposal boundary, above which a component may only propose. A chapter-opening stack with no level highlighted marks a governance chapter. The permission-loop rate shown is one implementation.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Five stacked horizontal bands labeled from top to bottom Brain (learned proposers, intent model 2 hertz, chunk renewal 20 hertz, setpoints 40 hertz), Nervous System (permission path, lease, fallback, a 1 kilohertz loop in one implementation), Body (drives, transmission, windings, rails, current loop 10 to 25 kilohertz), Boundary (register write and sensor capture, event-timed), and World (contact, friction, payload, people, continuous). A wider gap between Brain and Nervous System is labeled proposal boundary, above may only propose, below may command. A dashed outline enclosing all five bands is labeled governance, lifecycle envelope, Part IV, no control rate. Chapter numbers sit at the right edge of each band: Brain 3, 6, 8 to 11; Nervous System 4, 12, 13; Body 2; Boundary 1, 8; World 5, 7.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nDeploying capable physical AI requires balancing expressive policy proposals with deterministic execution guarantees. This separation of concerns does not demand rejecting every imperfect proposal out of hand; real-time intervention mechanisms can project a trajectory onto safe constraints or smoothly blend corrections, preserving task progress while respecting physical invariants. Systems evaluation must therefore disentangle what the learned proposer achieves in isolation, how effectively the integrated machine executes under routine supervisory filtering, and whether the fallback mechanisms themselves introduce secondary hazards.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The minimal-intervention optimization filters that project a proposal onto its constraints are developed in @sec-enforcement-minimal-intervention.\n:::\n\nDistributing responsibilities across these five levels gives the rest of the book its structure. It also gives a way to diagnose real failures by asking which level failed, rather than reducing every physical malfunction to a deficiency in neural network weights.\n\n## When the Responsibilities Fail Together {#sec-boundary-when-fails}\n\nA warehouse model that recognizes an obstacle too late cannot recover the clearance already consumed. In a road vehicle, the same timing problem can combine with tracking errors, delayed intervention, and missing redundancy. The 2018 Tempe collision provides a documented example of these interacting failures; it does not establish that one prescribed hardware architecture would have prevented them. The National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) attributed the crash primarily to the vehicle operator&#x27;s distraction. The design choices traced in this section explain why the automated system had made that single, unmonitored person the last line of defense.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE TEMPE AND CRUISE RECORDS (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @tbl-01-law-lineage and @sec-boundary-when-fails (Tempe timeline,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          action suppression, braking cap, Cruise pullover, checkpoint).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Print the investigators' own values once, and derive the travel</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          during action suppression from the two recorded speeds.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    5.6 s first detection; 43.2 mph at 1.2 s before impact; 1 s</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          suppression ending at 0.2 s and 40.5 mph; takeover at 0.02 s;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          18.7 m during suppression; 7 m/s^2 (0.71 g) chosen cap against</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          0.92 g (9.0 m/s^2) capability; Cruise about 20 ft (6.1 m) at up</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          to 7.7 mph.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     cited literature (NTSB/HAR-19/03 and the Quinn Emanuel</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          report). Suppression travel is Scenarios.TempeActionSuppression,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the mean of the two recorded speeds over the suppression (trapezoid</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          rule); no counterfactual stop is computed.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import Literature, Scenarios from mlsysim.core.units import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, fmt_acceleration, fmt_math, check</p>\n<p>class IncidentRecord: \"\"\"Investigator-reported values for the Tempe (2018) and Cruise (2023) cases.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>SUPP = Scenarios.TempeActionSuppression</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">category: literature. NTSB/HAR-19/03 (2019), table pp. 15-16 and p. 13</h1>\n<p>t_detect = 5.6 <em> second  # first detection, before impact t_recognize = 1.2 </em> second  # emergency recognized and suppression begins, before impact v_recognize = SUPP.speed  # recorded speed at that instant t_suppress = SUPP.delay  # action-suppression period (p. 13) t_supp_end = 0.2 <em> second  # suppression ends, before impact v_supp_end = SUPP.speed_at_delay_end  # recorded speed at that instant t_takeover = 0.02 </em> second  # operator takes the wheel, before impact</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">category: literature. NTSB/HAR-19/03, p. 13 and footnote 33</h1>\n<p>a_cap = 7.0 <em> meter / second</em>*2  # ADS emergency threshold and braking cap (chosen by the developer) g_capable = 0.92  # police-determined average deceleration capability in the crash area, in g</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">category: literature. Quinn Emanuel report to the Cruise boards (2024), p. 10</h1>\n<p>d_drag = 20.0 <em> foot  # approximate distance moved during the pullover v_drag = 7.7 </em> mph  # maximum speed during the pullover</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>g0 = (1 * standard_gravity).to(meter / second<strong>2) d_suppress = SUPP.result.delay_travel.to(meter) a_cap_g = (a_cap / g0).to(\"dimensionless\").magnitude a_capable = SUPP.deceleration.to(meter / second</strong>2) d_drag_m = d_drag.to(meter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs((t_recognize - t_supp_end - t_suppress).to(second).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"Suppression must span the two table rows.\") check(abs(a_cap_g - 0.71) &lt; 5e-3, f\"NTSB states the cap as 0.71 g, got {a_cap_g}.\") check(a_capable &gt; a_cap, \"The chosen cap must sit below the vehicle's capability.\") check(abs((a_capable - g_capable <em> g0).to(meter / second</em>*2).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The scenario brakes at the police-measured capability.\") check(abs(d_suppress.magnitude - 18.7) &lt; 0.05, f\"Suppression travel is about 18.7 m, got {d_suppress}.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_detect_str = fmt_latency(t_detect, unit=second, precision=1) t_suppress_str = fmt_latency(t_suppress, unit=second, precision=0) t_recognize_math = fmt_math(rf\"\\ImpactInstant-{fmt(t_recognize, unit=second, precision=1)}\\text{{ s}}\") t_supp_end_math = fmt_math(rf\"\\ImpactInstant-{fmt(t_supp_end, unit=second, precision=1)}\\text{{ s}}\") t_takeover_math = fmt_math(rf\"\\ImpactInstant-{fmt(t_takeover, unit=second, precision=2)}\\text{{ s}}\") v_recognize_str = fmt_velocity(v_recognize, unit=mph, precision=1) v_supp_end_str = fmt_velocity(v_supp_end, unit=mph, precision=1) d_suppress_str = fmt_length(d_suppress, unit=meter, precision=1) a_cap_str = fmt_acceleration(a_cap, unit=meter / second<strong>2, precision=0) a_cap_g_str = fmt(a_cap_g, precision=2) g_capable_str = fmt(g_capable, precision=2) a_capable_str = fmt_acceleration(a_capable, unit=meter / second</strong>2, precision=1) d_drag_str = fmt_length(d_drag, unit=foot, precision=0) d_drag_m_str = fmt_length(d_drag_m, unit=meter, precision=1) v_drag_str = fmt_velocity(v_drag, unit=mph, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>On a March night in 2018, a modified Volvo XC90 operating Uber Advanced Technologies Group's developmental automated driving system (ADS) struck and fatally injured a pedestrian pushing a bicycle across a road in Tempe, Arizona [@ntsb2019uber]. The vehicle carried a roof-mounted lidar, forward and side radars, surround cameras, and GPS antennas, all routed to ADS compute and data-storage racks in the cargo bay (@fig-01-tempe-vehicle). The ADS first detected the pedestrian <code>[IncidentRecord.t_detect_str]</code> before impact, but detection alone did not provide a correct path prediction [@ntsb2019uber]. The failure ran through perception and path prediction, a response rule that withheld braking, and a human operator the design relied on as its last countermeasure.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Reading the Tempe Record</strong>: Times in this section count backward from impact at $\\Time$, as the event table of NTSB/HAR-19/03 does, and speeds are those the ADS itself recorded [@ntsb2019uber]. The report also cautions that communication delay leaves unclear exactly when the planned slowdown began. Reading each time as an interval before impact, rather than as a clock time, is what lets it be converted into distance traveled at the recorded speed, the conversion this section applies.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h3\">Tracking history loss under classification churn</h3>\n<p>Despite continuing to track the detected object, the ADS did not brake in response to it because its classification kept shifting among vehicle, bicycle, and other before <code>[IncidentRecord.t_recognize_math]</code> [@ntsb2019uber]. Whenever the classification changed, the ADS path predictor did not use the object's earlier locations, and for an \"other\" object it could assume a static path. The loss of earlier locations matters because successive positions provide evidence of motion across the roadway; a changing semantic label must not silently discard that evidence. Because each relabeling shortened the track, the resulting path predictions did not place the pedestrian in the SUV's lane until <code>[IncidentRecord.t_recognize_math]</code> [@ntsb2019uber].</p>\n<p>: <strong>Tracking-History Loss</strong>: The NTSB record states only that a changed classification caused the predictor to omit the object's earlier locations; it does not describe a Kalman-filter reset or a zero-velocity initialization, so neither should be read into it [@ntsb2019uber]. The consequence follows from the omission alone. Each relabeling shortened the evidence window from which motion is estimated, so an object crossing the lane began again, after each change, as one with no recorded history of crossing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h3\">Action suppression and lost stopping distance</h3>\n<p>The ADS included an action-suppression interval intended to reduce responses to false alarms while the system checked the situation and allowed operator intervention [@ntsb2019uber]. That software choice directly consumed time available for a physical response: at <code>[IncidentRecord.t_recognize_math]</code>, with the vehicle traveling at <code>[IncidentRecord.v_recognize_str]</code>, the ADS recognized an emergency and entered its one-second action-suppression period with braking withheld. At <code>[IncidentRecord.t_supp_end_math]</code>, suppression ended with the vehicle at <code>[IncidentRecord.v_supp_end_str]</code>. The ADS then initiated a plan for gradual slowdown and sounded an alert; it did not request emergency braking. The NTSB notes that communication delay leaves unclear whether that slowdown began before the operator disengaged the ADS at <code>[IncidentRecord.t_takeover_math]</code> [@ntsb2019uber].</p>\n<p>The first term of the warehouse stopping budget applies directly. Averaging the two recorded speeds over the <code>[IncidentRecord.t_suppress_str]</code> of suppression, the vehicle traveled about <code>[IncidentRecord.d_suppress_str]</code> while emergency braking was withheld, distance that no later action could recover. The speed still fell during that second because a slowdown for the planned right turn at the next intersection was already under way; what the suppression withheld was the emergency response [@ntsb2019uber]. The emergency itself was defined by a chosen limit. The ADS treated a situation as an emergency when avoiding a collision required more than <code>[IncidentRecord.a_cap_str]</code> (<code>[IncidentRecord.a_cap_g_str]</code> g) of deceleration or a jerk beyond its limit, and it capped its own automated braking at that deceleration. Police determined that the vehicle could decelerate at an average of <code>[IncidentRecord.g_capable_str]</code> g, about <code>[IncidentRecord.a_capable_str]</code>, on that road surface [@ntsb2019uber]. The difference between a chosen permission limit and a physical one is the distinction the book's numbers keep throughout. The calculation stops short of a counterfactual stop, because the report does not establish when a different design would have begun braking. Detection, correct path prediction, hazard recognition, and physical braking are distinct events; reporting the first detection time alone conceals the failures between them.</p>\n<p>↪ The stopping envelope, which charges every interval of delayed braking against the clear distance, is developed in @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Jerk Limit</strong>: Jerk, met in the stopping notebook as the rate of change of deceleration, is measured in m/s³. A jerk limit makes a brake ramp its deceleration up instead of stepping it, which spares occupants, loads, and transmissions a shock but costs stopping distance, because full deceleration arrives later. The warehouse machine's card carries a jerk bound for the same reason, and the machine's finished stopping budget pays for the ramp.</p>\n<p><strong>Context</strong>: On March 18, 2018, a developmental ADS on a 2017 Volvo XC90 struck and killed a pedestrian crossing a road in Tempe, Arizona [@ntsb2019uber].</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>: In this sequence, classification changes discarded the object's tracking history, and the one-second suppression withheld braking until only a gradual slowdown was planned. The design did not use maximum braking solely to mitigate an unavoidable collision [@ntsb2019uber].</p>\n<p><strong>Impact</strong>: The vehicle struck and fatally injured the pedestrian. The late gradual-slowdown plan could not recover the lost stopping opportunity.</p>\n<p><strong>Response</strong>: The NTSB determined that the probable cause was the vehicle operator's failure to monitor the driving environment and the ADS because she was visually distracted throughout the trip by her personal cell phone. It found three contributing factors at Uber ATG, all a consequence of an inadequate safety culture: inadequate safety risk assessment procedures, ineffective oversight of vehicle operators, and a lack of adequate mechanisms for addressing operators' automation complacency. The impaired pedestrian's crossing outside a crosswalk and the Arizona Department of Transportation's insufficient oversight of automated vehicle testing also contributed. The NTSB further found that deactivating the Volvo collision warning and automatic emergency braking, which Uber ATG had done over possible radar interference, removed a layer of safety redundancy, and it recommended stronger safety risk management for testing automated vehicles [@ntsb2019uber].</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: Classification uncertainty, a delayed response rule, and reliance on an inattentive operator can combine. The design made the operator the primary countermeasure in an emergency, yet no mechanism monitored her attention in real time: authority rested on evidence nobody collected. A design needs timely hazard assessment and a response that can mitigate impact when avoidance is no longer possible; the investigation does not prescribe a particular hardware architecture.</p>\n<p>The investigation supports a broader architectural requirement. A developmental ADS needs safety redundancy and a timely mitigation path even when perception or path prediction fails, and simulation tests by Volvo that the NTSB cites suggest that the factory emergency braking might have prevented or mitigated this collision. The report does not establish when a separate permission path would have triggered or where it would have stopped the vehicle.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Safety-Critical Standards</strong>: Three standards divide the ground. ISO 26262 addresses hazards caused by faults in a vehicle's electrical and electronic systems [@iso26262]; ISO 21448, safety of the intended functionality (SOTIF), addresses hazards that arise when a function works as designed but performs insufficiently, as perception can [@iso21448]; and UL 4600 sets out the safety case for autonomous products with no human driver to fall back on [@ul4600]. Tempe falls on the SOTIF side, since the recorded failures were in classification and path prediction rather than in a faulted component, and none of the three prescribes a processor architecture. @Sec-release-standards-map maps the standards that govern the running machine.</p>\n<p>Tempe illustrates failure <em>before</em> impact, where classification changes and a software suppression delay prevented timely braking. The 2023 Cruise robotaxi incident marks the opposite pole <em>after</em> impact. A hit-and-run driver's car threw a pedestrian into the path of the driverless vehicle, which braked hard but struck her and stopped. It then began a pullover toward a minimal-risk condition and, because its sensing did not detect a person beneath the vehicle, moved about <code>[IncidentRecord.d_drag_str]</code> (<code>[IncidentRecord.d_drag_m_str]</code>) at up to <code>[IncidentRecord.v_drag_str]</code> while she was pinned beneath it. This account comes from a review commissioned by the company's boards, whose focus was the company's disclosures to regulators [@quinnemanuel2024cruise].</p>\n<p>↪ The Cruise pullover, and the fallback rule that forbids renewed motion while the space beneath the vehicle is unverified, are developed in @sec-enforcement-four-rungs.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Minimal-Risk Condition</strong>: In automated driving, the stable, usually stopped, state a system brings a vehicle to when a trip cannot or should not continue. Reaching it can itself require motion, as a pullover to the curb does, and in the Cruise case that motion was the harm. A fallback trajectory therefore needs the same permission check as any other proposal, judged against fresh evidence about the space it will move through.</p>\n<p>Neither failure reduces to an inaccurate model. The first spent stopping distance before braking began, and no later action could recover it. The second chose motion after the impact without fresh evidence about the space it moved through. Together they show what must hold before the machine acts and what a fallback may do once harm has begun. Both also had to be reconstructed afterward from records of what the machine perceived and when, so accountability, like safety, depends on the path that authorizes motion.</p>\n<p>↪ Who answered for the Cruise collision, and the decisions behind an admitted action that no per-tick log records, are developed in @sec-responsibility-who-answered.</p>\n<p>Verify how the Tempe mechanisms connect to the architecture:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Classification changes and path prediction</strong>: Explain how classification changes caused the ADS path predictor to omit earlier object locations, despite continued tracking.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Software suppression vs. kinematic clearance</strong>: Distinguish the <code>[IncidentRecord.t_recognize_math]</code> recognition time from the one-second suppression period, and explain why the gradual-slowdown plan at <code>[IncidentRecord.t_supp_end_math]</code> could not restore the time already spent.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Safety redundancy</strong>: Explain why disabling factory collision warning and emergency braking removed a layer of protection from a developmental ADS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h2\">Where Safety, Security, and Accountability Meet</h2>\n<p>Tempe and Cruise both turned on what the machine knew when it moved or failed to brake, and both had to be reconstructed afterward from what it had recorded. The same three questions (whether the evidence behind a motion was sound, whether anything could refuse it, and whether the decision can be traced) also describe an attack and an audit. In enterprise software and distributed systems, safety, security, and accountability usually live in separate tiers. Security defends network boundaries with cryptographic tokens, reliability frameworks mitigate component failures through retries and circuit breakers, and accountability records transactions to append-only databases. In physical AI, all three converge on the causal boundary, the interface where software commands meet physical actuators. Consider the running machine crossing a warehouse aisle. It needs a valid stopping decision whether its forward cameras are obscured by dirt, an attacker has injected spoofed packets onto its internal Controller Area Network (CAN) bus, or a vision-language-action policy has hallucinated an unobstructed path. In each case the hazard is kinetic, a collision with racking or a worker. Because momentum cannot be rolled back like a failed database transaction, admission control cannot simply confirm who dispatched a command. It must determine whether executing that command now would violate the machine's physical limits.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ A SLICK FLOOR AGAINST THE CREDIBLE DECELERATION (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: the slick-floor paragraph of @sec-boundary-safety-convergence.\n# │ Goal:    Print the illustrative friction pair and check the two claims the\n# │          paragraph makes about it.\n# │ Show:    the aisle&#x27;s 0.60 dry and 0.05 under a clear oil film; braking at\n# │          the traction limit grows twelvefold; 0.05 falls below the card&#x27;s\n# │          0.204 premise.\n# │ How:     Scenarios.HydraulicFluidSlickTraction (TractionLossModel) against\n# │          the running machine&#x27;s credible deceleration.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import Scenarios\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_math, check\n\nclass FloorTraction:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Illustrative friction drop under the running machine (scenario inputs).&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    SLICK = Scenarios.HydraulicFluidSlickTraction\n    mu_before = SLICK.mu_before  # category: illustrative (AISLE.mu_dry, tire on dry concrete)\n    mu_after = SLICK.mu_after  # category: illustrative (AISLE.mu_oil_film, tire on a clear oil film)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    traction = SLICK.result\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(traction.braking_distance_ratio &gt; 4, &quot;Prose says braking distance grows severalfold.&quot;)\n    check(not traction.premise_holds, &quot;Prose says the slick friction falls below the card&#x27;s premise.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    mu_before_math = fmt_math(fmt(mu_before, precision=2))\n    mu_after_math = fmt_math(fmt(mu_after, precision=2))\n    braking_ratio_str = fmt(traction.braking_distance_ratio, precision=0)\n]```\n\nAn ordinary environmental change challenges this boundary as directly as an attack. The running machine&#x27;s credible deceleration of `[StoppingBudget.a_brake_str]` rests on the premise, stated on its card, that the floor supplies a friction coefficient of at least `[MachineCard.mu_all_str]`. When the floor cannot supply the friction the brakes ask for, traction rather than the brakes caps deceleration, and braking distance grows in inverse proportion to the available friction. A film of spilled hydraulic fluid can drop tire-floor friction from about `[FloorTraction.mu_before_math]` on the aisle&#x27;s dry concrete to `[FloorTraction.mu_after_math]` (an illustrative pair). A vehicle that brakes at the traction limit would see its braking distance grow by a factor of `[FloorTraction.braking_ratio_str]`. The running machine asks far less of a dry floor than it supplies, but `[FloorTraction.mu_after_math]` falls below its premise. Its credible deceleration therefore no longer holds, and every clearance envelope and speed profile computed from that deceleration a moment earlier is invalid. The learned planner, processing camera streams that do not reveal the transparent slick, may continue to output an aggressive velocity command with high nominal confidence. Safety verification cannot treat the model&#x27;s internal confidence as proof of physical feasibility. The machine needs independent, real-time evidence from the plant and its surroundings to detect the divergence. Such evidence includes wheel-slip telemetry, disagreement between commanded and inertial-measurement-unit (IMU) acceleration, and motor current transients. The machine can then restrict motion using a response feasible under the updated traction estimate. If the machine has already consumed the clearance that response needs, detection can support harm mitigation but cannot establish collision avoidance.\n\nSecurity threats target this same permission machinery by corrupting the evidence used to authorize motion. An adversary does not need to crack cryptographic keys or modify model weights to destabilize a physical agent; distorting the sensory inputs that inform admission decisions is sufficient. Projecting structured light patterns onto camera lenses, spoofing ultra-wideband (UWB) localization beacons, or injecting rogue packets into sensor buses can deceive the controller into approving destructive commands, even while the neural network executes its inference pipeline without numerical fault. Isolating the proposer from the permission path offers little defense if both subsystems ingest the same corrupted telemetry. Securing the causal boundary therefore requires validating the evidence itself. The validation consists of cross-checking heterogeneous sensor streams (for example, corroborating camera-based visual odometry against wheel encoders and inertial sensors), enforcing cryptographic message authentication across bus transceivers, and maintaining fail-operational plausibility checks that reject sensor readings deviating from physical conservation laws.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ A well-formed packet that carries a false measurement becomes a fault trial in the injection campaign developed in @sec-verification-transduction-attacks.\n:::\n\nEven when sensory evidence is authentic and uncorrupted, authorizing a physical action differs from authorizing a digital one; in digital systems, a cryptographic capability token or access control list entry grants authority to modify a file regardless of processor load or ambient conditions. In physical AI, **physical authority is state-dependent**. Cryptographic credentials establish identity and organizational rights to request an action, but physical admissibility depends entirely on the instantaneous operational state of the machine and its workspace. A joint velocity command of 2 rad/s may represent a routine transit when an industrial manipulator operates in open free space, yet that identical command becomes unacceptable if the arm is within 2 mm of a mechanical hard stop or a human coworker. Evaluating physical authority requires testing the proposed action against instantaneous kinematics, payload inertia, motor thermal headroom, and state estimation uncertainty. Admission control must also remain coupled to an executable fallback. If the permission gate rejects a proposed command because the machine approaches an operational boundary, the controller must immediately dispatch a dynamically feasible contingency maneuver, such as controlled regenerative braking or a compliant hold that yields to contact. The maneuver halts or redirects the plant without exceeding mechanical stress limits.\n\nAccountability demands an audit trail that reconstructs the causal chain connecting sensory evidence to physical outcomes. When an embodied machine causes a collision or near-miss, post-incident analysis requires timestamp and clock-conversion uncertainty small enough to distinguish the relevant sensing, admission, dispatch, and physical-response events across multiple data streams. These streams span the raw observations ingested by the planner and the action proposed by the learned policy, alongside the state estimates and dynamic constraints evaluated by the admission gate, the filtered command dispatched to the motor drives, and the measured response from joint encoders and force-torque sensors. Command logs record the intended trajectory, while sensor telemetry measures the plant&#x27;s actual mechanical response. Recording dispatched setpoints alone does not confirm that an actuator executed them; an open-circuit fault, a tripped thermal breaker, or mechanical gearbox jamming will cause the physical plant to diverge immediately from commanded behavior. Both streams must be logged against synchronized hardware clocks (such as the IEEE 1588 Precision Time Protocol) so that investigators can distinguish between a planning failure, an admission gate error, and an electromechanical actuator breakdown. Reconstruction is necessary but does not by itself say who answers for an action. The decisions that set the machine&#x27;s envelope, its staffing, and its permission to operate are made before any tick, and they have to be recorded on purpose (@sec-responsibility-many-hands).\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ **Protocol:** The IEEE 1588 Precision Time Protocol exchange that aligns these clocks is developed in @sec-appendix-systems-ptp.\n:::\n\nSafety, security, and accountability ultimately converge on three unified demands at the permission path guarding the causal boundary:\n\n1. **Fresh, authenticated physical state**: The permission gate must operate on telemetry fresh enough to bound physical uncertainty and robust enough against tampering or degradation to justify admitting an action.\n2. **Deterministic authority to intervene**: The admission mechanism needs uncompromised physical authority to veto or clamp infeasible proposals and inject safe contingency maneuvers, regardless of the learned model&#x27;s internal confidence or identity.\n3. **Synchronized causal telemetry**: The system must keep an immutable record of what was proposed, what state was evaluated, what commands were dispatched, and how the machine responded.\n\nThe Tempe and Cruise incidents map onto these demands. Tempe&#x27;s predictor discarded evidence it already had, and the factory emergency braking that could have intervened independently had been disabled; Cruise moved without fresh evidence about the space beneath it. Both investigations then depended on the third demand, the recorded telemetry from which the events were reconstructed. The permission path is therefore where a physical machine&#x27;s safety, security, and accountability obligations are discharged, and anchoring probabilistic proposals to checks against physical limits is what lets learned capability operate within justified bounds. What those checks must respect, and why, is the subject of four laws.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The operational log that survives physical failure, from which an investigator reconstructs each admitted action, is developed in @sec-intervention-trustworthy-logs.\n:::\n\n## The Four Bedrock Laws {#sec-boundary-bedrock-laws}\n\nEvery check on the permission path answers to constraints that neither a learned policy nor a software supervisor can negotiate away. Whether the warehouse machine is braking for a coworker in an aisle, pressing a spring-latched cage door, or carrying an unmodeled payload, it must respect the mechanical limits of its plant, adapt to dynamic environments altered by its own motion, and arbitrate between speculative model outputs and physically safe actuation. Four bedrock laws organize these engineering responsibilities, each anchoring a major part of this book:\n\n1. **The first law: irreversibility.** Software cannot roll back physical consequences. In pure digital systems, an erroneous transaction can be aborted and state restored from a checkpoint. In physical space, however, work performed on a plant is thermodynamic and permanent. A motor command that collides with a fixture, tears a cable, or fractures a fragile payload cannot be retracted. Every admissibility check must therefore complete before the machine consumes its remaining clearance margin. As the stopping distance budget demonstrates, processing latency directly expends physical clearance, forcing the mechanical actuators to brake over an ever-shrinking distance. Reversing a drive voltage may eventually return an arm to its starting coordinates, but it cannot mend cracked structural material or recover energy lost to friction and heat. Part I translates this physical reality into kinetic budgets for the robot&#x27;s body and deterministic timing constraints for its low-level controllers.\n2. **The second law: endogenous data.** In classic offline machine learning, data samples are drawn independently from a stationary distribution. In embodied systems, data generation is endogenous. Every control action alters the physical state and determines what sensors will observe next. If an arm nudges an object during an approach, the object shifts; if a mobile base drifts off its nominal path, the subsequent visual frames deviate from the training demonstrations. Single-step prediction accuracy on an offline benchmark cannot guarantee closed-loop behavior, because minor execution errors compound across the task horizon, the covariate shift met with the closed causal loop (@sec-boundary-tensors-to-silicon). Part II frames data collection, model training, and policy evaluation as a continuous improvement cycle: diagnose behavioral failures, curate targeted recovery demonstrations, retrain candidate policies, and systematically evaluate both performance gains and unintended regressions. Empirical evidence from past rollouts bounds what can be claimed about present capability; only qualified new experience can expand that envelope.\n3. **The third law: proposal is not permission.** A learned model operates only as a proposer, never as an unconstrained executor. An architecturally independent permission path (a runtime safety filter or an assurance monitor, for example) must arbitrate every proposed control command before that command reaches hardware actuators. Granting permission requires three prerequisites: fresh state estimates from verified sensors, an explicit check that the command respects every physical safety envelope, and an immediately executable fallback maneuver if the proposal is delayed, missing, or unsafe. This separation of authority must remain robust against system faults, so a runaway or stalled model thread must never be allowed to delay, starve, or corrupt the deterministic safety logic guarding the plant. Part III constructs the real-time execution runtime around this division between speculative proposal and independent, bounded permission.\n4. **The fourth law: permission must be earned.** An embodied machine earns permission to operate only as far as empirical evidence supports its performance under stated conditions. Flawless execution across a handful of laboratory trials does not justify autonomy across diverse surface friction, variable payloads, degraded lighting, or sensor degradation. Every deployment safety case must explicitly demarcate the system&#x27;s operational design domain (ODD), articulate the environmental assumptions underlying those boundaries, and specify fail-safe degradation policies whenever runtime conditions drift beyond verified bounds. The 2018 Tempe collision showed the cost of ignoring this boundary. The system architecture relied on an in-vehicle safety operator as the primary emergency countermeasure, yet lacked any mechanism to monitor whether the operator was actually attending to the roadway [@ntsb2019uber]. Part IV formalizes command authority, fault injection testing, and release criteria so that epistemic and physical uncertainty bound what the machine is permitted to do.\n\n: **Operational Design Domain (ODD)**: The automated-driving term for the stated environmental and operating conditions (roadway types, speed ranges, weather, lighting) under which a system is designed to work, and the starting scope of a UL 4600 safety case [@ul4600]. This book applies the concept to the payloads, surface friction, operating speeds, and disturbances covered by the machine&#x27;s empirical testing. Operating outside that tested domain has no evidence behind it, which is why @sec-data-curse-dimensionality-recoverability-geometry organizes the operating domain as an explicit scenario table against which collected training data are audited. \n\nOf these four, only the first is a physical law. Irreversibility follows from Newtonian mechanics and thermodynamics. Work performed on a plant transfers energy and raises entropy, and no software architecture can undo that transition. The remaining three are engineering design principles, distilled from decades of systems engineering, industrial robotics, and aerospace failures. A team could build an architecture that violates them, for example by piping unvalidated network outputs straight to motor amplifiers, but it would incur severe and predictable risks. The book elevates all four to bedrock laws because it treats them as mandatory constraints for any production embodied system.\n\nNone of these three architectural principles is entirely novel. Each has a rich foundation in computer systems, control theory, and safety-critical engineering. @Tbl-01-law-lineage maps this book&#x27;s terminology to the established literature, alongside the canonical sources and documented real-world failures each principle distills, including the Tempe and Cruise incidents. Where the book&#x27;s terms introduce tighter operational precision, as when tying state freshness directly to the dynamic stopping budget, the book retains specialized systems phrasing. When searching broader engineering literature, the classical terms serve as the standard index.\n\n| Book&#x27;s law or term                                                         | Kind             | Established name in the field                                             | Canonical source                                               | Documented failure it distills                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| First law: irreversibility (the causal boundary)                           | Physical         | Irreversible work on the plant; the reaction-plus-braking stopping budget | Mechanics; @sec-body-kinetic-momentum                          | Tempe (2018): the one-second action suppression let the vehicle travel about `[IncidentRecord.d_suppress_str]` with emergency braking withheld [@ntsb2019uber]                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| Second law: endogenous data                                                | Design principle | Covariate shift; compounding error in imitation learning                  | Ross, Gordon, and Bagnell [@ross2011reduction]                 | A learning result rather than an accident: a cloned policy&#x27;s errors can compound with the task horizon (@sec-data-covariate-shift)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| Third law: proposal is not permission (proposal boundary, permission path) | Design principle | Runtime assurance and its Simplex architecture; safety filter             | Sha [@sha2001using]; Hsu, Hu, and Fisac [@hsu2024safetyfilter] | Therac-25: software took over protection that hardware interlocks had provided [@leveson1993investigating]; Mars Pathfinder (1997): through priority inversion, lower-priority work delayed a deadline-critical bus task until the scheduler&#x27;s completion check reset the computer [@reeves1997pathfinder]                                                             |\n| Fresh evidence (an input to every permission check)                        | Design principle | Age of information                                                        | Kaul, Yates, and Gruteser [@kaul2012realtime]                  | While not a pure staleness failure, Tempe (2018) shows the neighboring hazard of discarded evidence: each object reclassification dropped historical tracks, forcing path prediction to compute trajectories on truncated sensor history [@ntsb2019uber]                                                                                                               |\n| Fourth law: permission must be earned                                      | Design principle | Assurance case; operational design domain (ODD)                           | UL 4600 [@ul4600]                                              | Tempe (2018): the design made the operator the primary countermeasure in an emergency, yet nothing monitored her attention in real time [@ntsb2019uber]; Cruise (2023): a post-collision pullover moved the vehicle without evidence that the space beneath it was clear, because its detection system did not identify the pedestrian there [@quinnemanuel2024cruise] |\n\n: **Lineage of the four laws**: Each law and key term of this book against the field&#x27;s established name, its canonical source, and a documented failure it distills. Only the first law is physical; the other three are design principles, which is why each has an independent literature. Runtime assurance enters with the proposal boundary, and @sec-boundary-history traces its lineage together with the safety filter and the assurance case. {#tbl-01-law-lineage tbl-colwidths=&quot;[20,10,20,16,34]&quot;}\n\nThe proposal boundary is where all four laws meet in one architecture. Irreversibility requires that every check complete before a command alters the plant, and endogenous data prevents offline training metrics from certifying a proposer in the closed loop. For these reasons, the third law (proposal is not permission) places that check on an independent permission path that the model cannot delay or corrupt. The fourth law (permission must be earned) then confines admitted proposals to the operating conditions that empirical evidence has verified. Scaling up model capacity does not relax these constraints; on the contrary, it intensifies physical pressure on the system. Although general machine learning methods deliver superior performance as compute expands [@sutton2019bitter], the running mobile machine must supply its onboard compute within a finite electrical and thermal budget. Every additional neural layer, attention head, or diffusion step draws against fixed physical allocations of memory bandwidth, electrical power, and thermal dissipation. Inference latency on the stopping path is travel that braking cannot recover. On the running machine at its drive limit, each millisecond costs `[StoppingBudget.d_per_ms_str]` of clearance, as the stopping notebook showed. This compute, thermal, and temporal ceiling forms the physical wall confronting every learned proposer.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The memory-bandwidth and latency bottlenecks confronting onboard learned proposers are developed in @sec-brain-cognitive-pipeline.\n:::\n\nFinally, rejecting an inadmissible command must have an explicit physical realization. In traditional software, an unhandled exception or failed assertion can simply terminate a thread. On a moving robot, halting a software process leaves physical momentum untouched. A refusal is meaningful only if the execution runtime immediately dispatches an admissible fallback (a dynamic braking maneuver, a compliant hold, or a controlled stop) that the physical body can safely execute within its remaining stopping clearance. Translating the four laws and their runtime mechanisms into an engineered system takes the four parts of this book, each producing records that later parts rely on.\n\n## The Architecture of This Book {#sec-boundary-book-architecture}\n\nMoving a learned proposal, such as the running machine&#x27;s trajectory to open a warehouse cage door, from an abstract task requirement to safe, justified physical operation requires a disciplined four-stage progression. The engineer must first bound what the hardware plant can physically achieve and characterize what the learned proposer has demonstrated under rigorous testing. The engineer must then build an isolated runtime monitor to intercept live proposals and establish the operational conditions under which continued execution remains justified. Each stage corresponds to one of the four laws. The system of records illustrated in @fig-01-spine formalizes this progression, capturing operating assumptions and empirical evidence so that downstream runtime decisions can inspect and rely on them. Conceptually, these records trace two converging pipelines. One strand establishes offline physical limits and verified competence; the second processes online sensor observations and real-time candidate actions. Both pipelines converge at the permission decision, where the system admits or rejects a proposed trajectory. In @sec-frontier-architectural-synthesis, this architecture synthesizes both strands to resolve the book&#x27;s central challenge of engineering dependable, high-performance physical systems out of unverified learned models and unforgiving mechanical hardware.\n\nA record is a machine-checkable artifact that downstream safety and runtime decisions can verify and trust. The initial stopping calculation (notebook \\ref{nbk-boundary-stopping-budget}) already sketches the first record in the chain: a limit record, with the six elements the reader guide introduces (nominal value, operating conditions, uncertainty, margin, runtime detector, and provenance). Its nominal value is the derived stopping distance under stated operating conditions of loaded payload mass, dry concrete, a stationary obstacle, and constant approach speed. Because those inputs are illustrative scalars rather than calibrated distributions, however, its uncertainty remains unquantified. The apparent buffer between the obstacle and the machine is therefore only an arithmetic difference between provisional figures rather than a verified safety margin. The calculation also lacks a runtime detector (no sensor yet monitors whether floor traction drops or cargo shifts outside the assumed envelope, @sec-body-limit-record-schema), and its provenance rests on baseline assumptions rather than calibrated hardware logs. An ungrounded calculation becomes operational engineering evidence only when all six fields are populated and backed by empirical data. Every numbered box across the four part bands in @fig-01-spine establishes a machine-checkable record of this rigor for a distinct architectural decision.\n\n::: {#fig-01-spine fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**The spine of the book**: Each part is led by one law, and each chapter of Parts I to IV except The Cognitive Brain (Chapter 3) produces a record that a later one consumes. What the plant can do and what the proposer has shown it can do run as one strand, what the proposer proposes now as another, and the two meet at the permission decision.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Flow diagram of sixteen record boxes, each labeled with its chapter number, on shaded bands for the four parts. In the Part I band, labeled Irreversibility, the limit record of Chapter 2 feeds the handoff record of Chapter 4, and a dashed arrow labeled Chapter 3, proposer limits, also enters the handoff record. The handoff record forks into two rows. The upper row, in the Part II band labeled Endogenous data, runs provenance record 5, policy manifest 6, and evaluation record 7. The lower row, in the Part III band labeled Proposal is not permission, runs observation contract 8, belief record 9, intent lease 10, and trajectory record 11. Both rows converge on the enforcement record of Chapter 12, which feeds the placement record of Chapter 13. An arrow then runs down to a lower row in the Part IV band, labeled Permission must be earned: authority record 14, fault record 15, release manifest 16, and approval record 17, which feeds the residual-claims register of Chapter 18 outside the bands. A separate arrow runs from the evaluation record around the top and right edges to the release manifest.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nPart I, The Machine Anatomy, translates physical irreversibility into rigid architectural limits. @Sec-body profiles the mechanical plant to quantify its physical budgets (measurement freshness, stopping distance, deliverable torque, winding heat, and supply voltage). @Sec-brain measures the throughput and inference latency of the learned proposer to determine what it can deliver before deadlines expire. @Sec-nervous links compute to the actuators across deterministic buses, structuring a permission path whose timing guarantees derive from the plant&#x27;s stopping requirements. Together, the limit record and handoff record make every physical and temporal assumption explicit. Part II, Teaching the Machine, confronts the reality that physical actions continuously alter future sensor observations. @Sec-data, @sec-training, and @sec-evaluation build the evidentiary chain required to collect demonstrations, train control policies, and bound policy performance to a declared operational design domain. Across this pipeline, the provenance record, policy manifest, and evaluation record document both the empirical competence demonstrated by the model and the operational boundaries where its statistical guarantees break down.\n\nPart III, Running the Machine, enforces at runtime the separation between proposing an action and permitting it to execute. From @sec-perception through @sec-placement, the chapters trace candidate commands through the online runtime. First, they qualify sensor measurements in observation contracts and maintain state estimates in a belief record; next, they bound a goal with an intent lease (a time-limited grant of a target, never of a path, and distinct from the chunk lease that bounds one motion segment); finally, they check candidate trajectories against real-time physical constraints before admission and dispatch, then test whether the hardware keeps the permission path independent. Part IV, Governing the Machine, enforces the principle that permission must be earned. @Sec-intervention, @sec-verification, and @sec-release govern how authority transfers between the machine and a human operator, how fault injection tests whether protective responses hold, and how deployment decisions are assembled into release manifests. @Sec-responsibility then names who answers for each decision behind an admitted action, tests whether each human role the design relies on is one a person can perform, and counts the people who bear the machine&#x27;s risk and appear in its records. Finally, @sec-frontier brings the framework to bear on an unfamiliar machine, synthesizing what an engineering team can construct and formally justify while identifying the residual risks that require further mitigation. Across these parts, the warehouse mobile manipulator introduced here provides a single concrete machine on which to ground each architectural decision. Its records render design assumptions fully inspectable, though an engineer must remember that assembling a record documents an explicit claim rather than guaranteeing its physical truth. The system record register in @sec-appendix-systems-record-register catalogs all sixteen records, noting their producing chapters and the specific invariants they bind, while the machine card in @sec-boundary-running-machine specifies the foundational physical parameters of the robot itself. Together, this four-stage route forms a repeatable discipline for making learned behavior dependable in physical space, guarding against the common misconceptions that arise when digital assumptions are carried across the causal boundary.\n\n## Fallacies and Pitfalls {#sec-boundary-fallacies-pitfalls}\n\nThe misconceptions that follow arise when assumptions from pure software engineering, statistical learning, or classical control theory are imported into embodied machines. In digital systems, an erroneous state can typically be trapped, rolled back, or restarted. Across the causal boundary, however, software outputs become physical forces that alter the world irrevocably. Preventing physical harm requires hard deterministic guarantees that learning objectives, human operators, and asynchronous recovery routines cannot provide on their own.\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n\n**Fallacy**: *Safety constraints can be trained into the policy.*\n\nEngineering teams often attempt to enforce physical safety by adding penalty terms, barrier penalties, or negative rewards to the training objective. While penalty shaping suppresses undesirable actions over the training distribution, it provides no mathematical guarantee on the actions emitted during deployment. Deep neural networks remain uncalibrated function approximators when exposed to out-of-distribution inputs, sensor degradation, or novel environmental dynamics. A statistical loss function optimizes expected performance, but safety requires hard invariants that hold on every control cycle. For this reason, deterministic safety enforcement must sit downstream of the policy, operating as an independent permission path that filters proposed commands before they reach actuator driver registers, exactly where the third law places the boundary gate.\n\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n\n**Pitfall**: *Treating human supervisory intervention as instantaneous in shared-autonomy systems.*\n\nIn shared-autonomy designs, such as a robotic surgical tool providing haptic guidance from a learned anatomical model, system designers sometimes treat the human operator as a failsafe catch-all. This assumption ignores the physiological limits of human reaction time, which ranges between 150 ms and 250 ms for simple tactile or visual reflexes and grows under cognitive load. Once console video and a handshake are added, the running machine&#x27;s card budgets an in-loop takeover at `[MachineCard.value_str[&quot;t_takeover_in&quot;]]`. Over that interval, an actuator running on a 1 kHz control loop can execute hundreds of unchecked cycles, generating enough force to tear soft tissue or damage a mechanical workpiece before the operator can intervene. Delegating authority to a human supervisor does not eliminate the machine&#x27;s capacity for harm during the supervisory lag. The permission path must enforce local force, velocity, and displacement envelopes autonomously, preserving the established physical envelopes under their operating assumptions without relying on timely human overrides.\n\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ AN INFERENCE STALL (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the pitfall on software latency in @sec-boundary-fallacies-pitfalls.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Print the stall the pitfall names from the scenario that also</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          prices its travel.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    a 100 ms inference hiccup, longer than the 60 ms chunk lease.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseInferenceHiccup (scenario assumption).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import Embodied, Scenarios from mlsysim.core.units import millisecond from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, check</p>\n<p>class InferenceStall: \"\"\"The stall in the latency pitfall (scenario assumption).\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>STALL = Scenarios.WarehouseInferenceHiccup t_stall = STALL.delay  # category: illustrative (inference stall) t_lease = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.t_lease  # category: chosen (chunk lease)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(t_stall &gt; t_lease, \"Pitfall: the stall outlasts the chunk lease, so the lease, not the proposer, must end the motion.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_stall_str = fmt_latency(t_stall, unit=millisecond, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Assuming software latency merely degrades performance gracefully.</em></p>\n<p>In purely digital systems, inference latency or scheduler jitter increases response time or degrades service quality. In physical systems, timing is a functional correctness requirement. If a learned policy stalls, whether from accelerator kernel contention, memory allocation pauses, or network serialization delays, allowing its previous command to persist across subsequent control cycles turns stale intent into physical danger. For example, a mobile manipulator that repeats its last forward-velocity command during a <code>[InferenceStall.t_stall_str]</code> inference hiccup can collide with an unmapped obstacle. Actuation commands must therefore carry bounded temporal validity, enforced by hardware or real-time software watchdogs. When that validity window (the chunk lease of the stopping budget) expires without a verified update, the low-level controller must begin a fallback within its established response budget. The selected stop or hold must remain feasible for the current mechanism, state, and surviving resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Assuming physical actuation is reversible through subsequent software compensation.</em></p>\n<p>Transactional software architectures rely on rollbacks and compensation routines to undo erroneous operations. In embodied systems, thermodynamics and materials science break this equivalence. If a robotic arm applies excessive torque to a workpiece, issuing a reverse motor command cannot unbend plastically deformed metal, repair fractured composite, or recover the electrical energy dissipated as heat in the motor windings ($\\MotorPhaseRmsCurrent^2 \\PhaseResistance$). Anomaly detection routines that react after physical contact can only truncate ongoing damage; they cannot undo the mechanical work already transferred across the causal boundary. Preventive verification, such as checking proposed trajectories against workspace boundaries, torque limits, and kinetic energy caps before dispatch, must serve as the primary defense. Reactive recovery strategies remain necessary for unexpected disturbances, but they function as a secondary backstop rather than a substitute for preventive enforcement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h2\">Summary</h2>\n<p>Physical AI converges two historically distinct engineering disciplines: classical robotics and modern machine learning. As learned models move progressively closer to direct actuator control, the boundary between digital computation and physical reality sharpens at the causal boundary, the interface where an admitted command becomes an irreversible physical effect. In purely software systems, state rollbacks and retries can recover from algorithmic faults; in the physical world, software cannot undo kinetic energy, momentum, or impact. Safe recovery requires an executable, physically feasible countermeasure.</p>\n<p>Physical dynamics also do not pause while neural networks compute. Because a machine continues to travel during sensing and processing delays, safety requires deterministic admission checks upstream of any consequential actuation. As the warehouse mobile manipulator's stopping budget and the Tempe collision show, prediction quality offers no protection once response delay has spent the clearance. The architecture resolves this tension by separating proposal from permission and giving an independent, bounded-latency permission path the authority to refuse. Four laws, one physical and three design principles, organize the framework the rest of the book develops, while the elementary stopping budget and its crossover speed form this chapter's quantitative core (@tbl-01-key-equations).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Equation</th><th>What it bounds or decides</th><th>Derived</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>$\\StoppingDistance = \\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay + \\dfrac{\\SpeedAtDetection^2}{2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration} \\le \\ClearDistance$</td><td>Whether a moving machine can stop within its observed clearance: distance traveled during response delay plus braking distance, prior to allowances added in later chapters</td><td>@eq-boundary-stopping-budget</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$\\StoppingCrossoverSpeed = 2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration\\PrebrakeDelay$</td><td>The crossover speed at which delay distance equals braking distance; below $\\StoppingCrossoverSpeed$, delay travel is larger, while above it, braking travel is larger</td><td>From @eq-boundary-stopping-budget, @sec-boundary-kinematic-roofline</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Key equations of the causal boundary</strong>: The elementary stopping budget and its crossover speed, with the place each is derived. {#tbl-01-key-equations tbl-colwidths=\"[34,46,20]\"}</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Takeaways</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Useful behavior requires an integrated machine</strong>: Physical AI combines learned decision-making, physical dynamics, and delegated mechanical authority. Delivering reliable autonomous operation requires co-designing task competence with physical body limits, real-time compute runtimes, and the operational evidence supporting deployment.</li>\n<li><strong>Response delay consumes physical margin</strong>: The elementary stopping budget $\\StoppingDistance = \\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay + \\SpeedAtDetection^2/(2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration) \\le \\ClearDistance$ partitions stopping distance into latency-induced travel and mechanical braking. Below the crossover speed $\\StoppingCrossoverSpeed = 2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration\\PrebrakeDelay$, response delay dominates stopping distance; above $\\StoppingCrossoverSpeed$, braking deceleration dominates. For the warehouse mobile manipulator at its drive limit, pre-braking travel adds <code>[StoppingBudget.blind_share_str]</code> to braking distance. The decisive delay belongs to whichever path triggers the protective response, which can operate independently of the learned proposer's inference time.</li>\n<li><strong>Proposal and permission serve distinct architectural roles</strong>: The learned proposer (the Brain) generates goal-directed actions, while an independent permission path admits, modifies, or rejects them before they cross the causal boundary. Safety enforcement relies on validated sensor inputs, physical kinematic envelopes, strict real-time deadlines, and verifiable fallback maneuvers.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence governs safe operation and systematic refinement</strong>: Four laws structure this book. Irreversibility is a physical law; the other three (endogenous data, proposal is not permission, and permission must be earned) are design principles that translate covariate shift, runtime assurance, and the assurance case into systems practice. Together they link physical hardware, embodied learning, real-time execution, and operational governance, establishing verified service within defined operational envelopes while identifying the targeted evaluations needed to expand them.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>Before judging whether a proposed action is right, an engineer should establish what physical response would remain if the proposal were wrong, stale, or never arrived, and whether the machine still holds the clearance, the time, and the authority to carry that response out. A design that cannot answer from its own measured limits has handed safety to prediction quality, and the stopping budget showed that prediction quality cannot buy back clearance once delay has spent it. The answer therefore starts from what the plant can do under stated conditions, never from what the model believes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h2\">Historical Perspective and Further Reading</h2>\n<p>That plant-first discipline has deep roots, because moving learned models directly into the actuation loop creates severe timing and safety hazards that earlier engineering fields had to solve. The engineering principles that resolve these challenges originate in embedded systems, real-time control, and safety engineering. General-purpose computing treats execution time as a mere performance optimization, assuming that correctness depends solely on logical input-output relationships. Cyber-physical systems theory overturned this assumption, holding that physical dynamics and computational processes must be modeled jointly because a command dispatched at the wrong time is physically incorrect [@lee2008cyber]. This insight forms the foundation of the causal boundary. Similarly, separating command proposal from execution permission stems from runtime assurance architectures. The Simplex architecture demonstrated that an industrial control system could safely run an unverified, high-performance controller if paired with a certified baseline controller and deterministic switching logic capable of taking back the plant before safety boundaries were violated [@seto1998simplex; @sha2001using]. Modern safety filters in embodied machine learning generalize this design, validating neural network proposals against mathematical certificates of safe states (control barrier functions or reachability envelopes, which @sec-enforcement develops) before granting actuator permission [@hsu2024safetyfilter].</p>\n<p>Many foundational safety principles in physical systems emerged from forensic investigations of catastrophic failures. The Therac-25 radiation therapy disasters demonstrated the fatal peril of replacing mechanical interlocks with software routines [@leveson1993investigating]. When race conditions bypassed software checks, the machine administered lethal radiation overdoses, proving that software reliability does not imply system safety. Systems-theoretic safety subsequently recast accidents not as isolated component breakdowns, but as failures of the overall control structure to enforce safety constraints on the interactions among components, feedback loops, and human operators [@leveson2011engineering]. This shift led directly to the assurance case, a structured argument demonstrating that concrete evidence satisfies defined safety claims within explicit operating environments [@kelly1998arguing]. The UL 4600 standard adopted this structure as its central framework for autonomous products [@ul4600], abandoning arbitrary road-testing mileage in favor of verifiable, claim-based arguments. Public accident records, particularly the National Transportation Safety Board investigation of the 2018 Tempe autonomous vehicle collision [@ntsb2019uber], remain the field's most complete documentation of how safety arguments unravel when classification changes, a response delay chosen in software, and reliance on a human fallback interact in the field.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Cyber-physical systems</strong> [@lee2008cyber]. Lee articulates why classical computing abstractions fail when computation interacts with physical processes. General-purpose computer architectures deliberately abstract away time, treating execution speed as a performance consideration rather than a correctness specification. Lee demonstrates that for physical interactions, the timing of an actuation command is inseparable from its physical meaning, providing the conceptual foundation for treating the causal boundary as an explicit systems interface.</li>\n<li><strong>Runtime assurance</strong> [@sha2001using]. Sha's paper on the Simplex architecture is the place to see how the switching logic decides when to take the plant back from the unverified controller, and why that logic must be simpler than what it supervises. It is the pattern behind this book's separation of proposal from permission.</li>\n<li><strong>Therac-25</strong> [@leveson1993investigating]. Leveson and Turner's forensic analysis traces the specific race conditions and the development and reporting practices that let the overdoses recur, detail that the preceding overview omits. It remains the essential case study in why protective functions need justification independent of the software they supervise.</li>\n<li><strong>Systems-theoretic safety</strong> [@leveson2011engineering]. Leveson introduces the STAMP accident model, reframing safety as a continuous control problem rather than a collection of component failure probabilities. Accidents occur when the control structure fails to enforce safety constraints across component interactions, sensor delays, and feedback loops. This text provides the theoretical basis for enforcing safety invariants as dynamic runtime constraints rather than static design assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>The Tempe investigation</strong> [@ntsb2019uber]. The National Transportation Safety Board report on the fatal 2018 Tempe autonomous vehicle crash offers the most thorough empirical record of an embodied AI safety failure. The report details how classification changes that discarded tracking history, a one-second action suppression meant to limit responses to false alarms, the separate deactivation of the vehicle's factory emergency braking over possible radar interference, and reliance on an operator whose attention nothing monitored combined in the collision. The event timeline and sensor telemetry provide vital reading for anyone building real-time perception-action loops.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Chapter Connection</div>\n<p>A permission decision needs a physical limit against which to judge the proposed action. @Sec-body begins Part I with the five budgets the permission path must check before it commits torque (measurement freshness, stopping distance, deliverable torque, winding heat, and supply voltage) and asks which binds first for a given maneuver, under what stress, and on what evidence. Each budget ends as a limit record, the six-element form this chapter sketched from its stopping calculation. @Sec-brain then examines the learned proposer's capabilities and timing, and @sec-nervous uses both sets of constraints to design the handoff and independent permission path. The progression starts with what the machine can sustain and ends with how computation may safely request it.</p>\n</div>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\\part{key:vol4_anatomy}</code></pre>", "quizzes": []};
